WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:38.913
[Elon] This is a difficult
thing that we're doing.

00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:41.583
It's a hard thing,
it's a new thing,

00:00:41.624 --> 00:00:45.795
and you know, I'd hope
that you would cheer us on.

00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:49.507
[dramatic music]

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:19.913
[Mission Control] Three,
two, one, and lift off!

00:01:19.954 --> 00:01:22.791
[rocket roaring]

00:01:22.832 --> 00:01:24.167
[Elon] I think this is
a really good milestone

00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:25.627
for the future of space flight.

00:01:25.668 --> 00:01:28.338
You know, I think it's another
step towards the stars.

00:01:29.506 --> 00:01:33.051
[Narrator] Elon Musk is not
your average tech billionaire.

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:35.345
He's one of the most
intriguing entrepreneurs

00:01:35.386 --> 00:01:37.138
in business today.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:40.308
The man behind PayPal
is taking on the giants

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.811
of the aerospace and
motor industries,

00:01:42.852 --> 00:01:44.771
along with the oil corporations,

00:01:44.813 --> 00:01:47.524
the energy utility
companies, and winning.

00:01:48.858 --> 00:01:50.610
[Ashley] Elon Musk
is a visionary,

00:01:50.652 --> 00:01:53.404
someone who comes along
perhaps once a century,

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:56.866
who is actually changing
the way we function

00:01:56.908 --> 00:01:58.576
and the way we
look at the world.

00:01:59.536 --> 00:02:00.703
Back then,

00:02:00.745 --> 00:02:02.372
people were still afraid
to buy books online,

00:02:02.413 --> 00:02:04.874
so putting together a
financial services suite

00:02:04.916 --> 00:02:07.961
on the internet was a
rather revolutionary idea,

00:02:08.002 --> 00:02:10.088
and that company turned
out to be PayPal,

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:11.714
the online payment service.

00:02:11.756 --> 00:02:13.550
It's one thing to run a company

00:02:13.591 --> 00:02:15.468
that's worth a billion dollars.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.804
Elon Musk has four.

00:02:17.846 --> 00:02:20.473
My personal ideology is
kind of split right now

00:02:20.515 --> 00:02:23.393
between trying to be helpful
on Earth related stuff,

00:02:23.434 --> 00:02:24.811
which is sustainable energy,

00:02:24.853 --> 00:02:27.272
and then trying to
advance space technology,

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:29.858
so we can establish a
self-sustaining city on Mars.

00:02:31.526 --> 00:02:34.863
From AI, to disrupting
the car industry,

00:02:34.904 --> 00:02:36.197
to SolarCity.

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:38.366
I mean how many
other people can say,

00:02:38.408 --> 00:02:39.909
I have my own rocket company?

00:02:39.951 --> 00:02:40.743
Not many.

00:02:42.203 --> 00:02:44.247
- In terms of space,
Elon has completely changed

00:02:44.289 --> 00:02:45.707
how we think about everything.

00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:47.709
When you look at
what SpaceX has done

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:49.335
with their reusable rockets,

00:02:49.377 --> 00:02:53.339
we are seeing science fiction
of the 1950s coming to life.

00:02:53.381 --> 00:02:56.426
Rockets land as they
always were meant to do.

00:02:56.467 --> 00:02:58.553
It's really trying to land
on a postage stamp there.

00:02:58.595 --> 00:03:00.305
[Doug] Astonishingly difficult.

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:02.265
So, Mars does beckon.

00:03:03.224 --> 00:03:04.517
I am very convinced

00:03:04.559 --> 00:03:06.603
that this is gonna
happen in my lifetime.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.105
- If you look
at Tesla alone,

00:03:09.147 --> 00:03:10.899
has changed the way
that automobiles

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.692
will be forever in the future.

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:15.278
He has truly broken
down the wall

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.071
that was impossible to break,

00:03:17.113 --> 00:03:18.990
and now we're in
a situation where

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:20.617
all of the big car companies

00:03:20.658 --> 00:03:23.244
are switching massively
to electric cars,

00:03:23.286 --> 00:03:27.957
and that is a revolution
done by one man.

00:03:29.083 --> 00:03:30.460
[Sarah] He's almost like
a real life Iron Man

00:03:30.501 --> 00:03:31.711
in what he's doing.

00:03:31.753 --> 00:03:33.588
There's a lot more
that will happen

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.089
before his story is over.

00:03:35.131 --> 00:03:39.093
So, I think we just get
to watch, and be excited,

00:03:39.135 --> 00:03:40.595
and see what comes next.

00:03:42.805 --> 00:03:46.517
A lot of people think that
successful entrepreneurs

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:48.645
are just happened to be in the
right place at the right time

00:03:48.686 --> 00:03:50.605
with the right product
or right service.

00:03:50.647 --> 00:03:51.397
That's untrue.

00:03:52.482 --> 00:03:54.692
Why are they not
content with $25 million

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:56.236
when they sell
their first company,

00:03:56.277 --> 00:03:59.322
then go on to a second company
for another $50 million,

00:03:59.364 --> 00:04:02.492
or a third company when they
fail and go into another one.

00:04:02.533 --> 00:04:05.828
Serial entrepreneurs are driven.

00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:08.790
They're psychologically driven,

00:04:08.831 --> 00:04:11.668
and it's usually to do with
something in their childhood.

00:04:18.174 --> 00:04:22.637
[Narrator] Elon Reeve Musk
was born in Pretoria in 1971,

00:04:22.679 --> 00:04:25.890
the son of a Canadian mother
and South African father.

00:04:29.018 --> 00:04:30.728
Elon grew up in South Africa,

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:34.857
and he had a very
unfettered upbringing.

00:04:34.899 --> 00:04:37.694
He has said that he was
raised basically by books

00:04:37.735 --> 00:04:40.280
and occasionally nannies.

00:04:40.321 --> 00:04:43.157
His mother and father
have two other kids.

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:45.493
They're, he has a younger
brother and sister,

00:04:45.535 --> 00:04:48.830
Kimball and Tuska, but it
seemed like Elon didn't have

00:04:48.871 --> 00:04:50.331
a lot of adult supervision.

00:04:50.373 --> 00:04:54.585
He said that he was basically
out there building explosives,

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:58.881
and learning about conduction
with very little supervision.

00:04:59.924 --> 00:05:01.634
He had a passion
for engineering,

00:05:01.676 --> 00:05:04.053
and obviously physics
and the sciences as well.

00:05:07.598 --> 00:05:10.268
[Narrator] Musk attended
Waterkloof Primary School,

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:12.353
and after his parents divorced,

00:05:12.395 --> 00:05:15.273
nine year old Elon went
to live with his father.

00:05:17.442 --> 00:05:20.278
Elon at one stage
in his childhood,

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.113
after his parents divorced,

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:24.699
says that he felt sorry for
his dad living by himself,

00:05:24.741 --> 00:05:27.035
so he opted to go and
live with his dad.

00:05:27.076 --> 00:05:30.038
That, he later called
a huge mistake.

00:05:30.079 --> 00:05:31.414
He's been on record saying,

00:05:31.456 --> 00:05:33.207
he didn't have a great
relationship with his father.

00:05:33.249 --> 00:05:34.667
He's notably said some really,

00:05:34.709 --> 00:05:36.878
really hard things
about his dad.

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:40.548
It sounds like it was a
really awful situation.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:42.175
His father was an engineer,

00:05:42.216 --> 00:05:45.720
and Elon says that a lot
of his engineering nous

00:05:45.762 --> 00:05:47.430
came from his father,

00:05:47.472 --> 00:05:50.892
and his father had some
issues according to Elon,

00:05:50.933 --> 00:05:54.020
but what he did have
was a very incisive

00:05:54.062 --> 00:05:55.438
engineering background.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.815
As disturbing as
Elon's relationship

00:05:57.857 --> 00:06:00.401
with his dad seemed to be,

00:06:00.443 --> 00:06:03.571
Elon's relationship with his
mother appears to be very good

00:06:03.613 --> 00:06:05.406
and went from
strength to strength.

00:06:05.448 --> 00:06:08.201
I think his mother is probably
the ultimate optimist.

00:06:08.242 --> 00:06:09.744
She's lovely.

00:06:09.786 --> 00:06:11.954
She's really something.

00:06:11.996 --> 00:06:13.456
[people chattering]

00:06:13.498 --> 00:06:16.042
Open the New York Times,
there's Maye, it's great.

00:06:16.084 --> 00:06:17.752
I've been modeling
for 50 years, ha!

00:06:17.794 --> 00:06:22.298
So, you just have to just
keep on going, and be nice,

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:24.675
and available, and be surprised

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:26.928
when you suddenly become
mother of the bride,

00:06:26.969 --> 00:06:28.721
and then grandmother.

00:06:28.763 --> 00:06:30.640
Elon's Mom has been
very supportive of him

00:06:30.681 --> 00:06:31.849
over the years.

00:06:31.891 --> 00:06:33.184
She's constantly
outspoken on Twitter

00:06:33.226 --> 00:06:34.685
telling us the wonders of Elon,

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:36.896
and she's clearly
very proud of him.

00:06:36.938 --> 00:06:39.065
Maye Musk is an
incredible woman.

00:06:39.107 --> 00:06:40.858
At something like 69,

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:43.444
she's called an "It
Girl" by Vanity Fair.

00:06:43.486 --> 00:06:47.115
She's an in-demand model
who's doing campaigns

00:06:47.156 --> 00:06:49.909
for big major brands
all over the world.

00:06:49.951 --> 00:06:52.495
She's one of the most
glamorous older women

00:06:52.537 --> 00:06:53.830
you will ever see.

00:06:55.456 --> 00:06:59.502
Despite the fact that Elon
says he tried repeatedly,

00:06:59.544 --> 00:07:00.920
again and again and again

00:07:00.962 --> 00:07:04.465
to try and repair his
relationship with his father,

00:07:04.507 --> 00:07:06.342
at this stage he's
just given up,

00:07:06.384 --> 00:07:09.637
because he doesn't believe
his dad is capable of change.

00:07:09.679 --> 00:07:12.306
He's talked openly
about how as a child

00:07:12.348 --> 00:07:13.975
he spent a lot of time alone.

00:07:14.016 --> 00:07:16.269
He says he can still remember

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:18.563
the sound of an empty house,

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:20.356
footsteps far off
in the distance,

00:07:20.398 --> 00:07:22.525
and he never ever
wanted that again.

00:07:23.109 --> 00:07:26.404
[suspenseful music]

00:07:29.157 --> 00:07:33.244
My own view is the really
stellar entrepreneurs,

00:07:33.286 --> 00:07:35.663
their life experiences
particularly in childhood

00:07:35.705 --> 00:07:38.416
are the determinant of
their success later on.

00:07:38.458 --> 00:07:40.585
If you look at their early
childhood experiences,

00:07:40.626 --> 00:07:44.130
you will find a number
of adverse events.

00:07:44.172 --> 00:07:47.508
Substantial adverse events
like school bullying,

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:49.260
or being rejected.

00:07:49.302 --> 00:07:52.388
These events drive you, you
now need to prove something.

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:54.515
You wanna gain control
of a world again,

00:07:54.557 --> 00:07:57.226
you didn't have control
of when you were younger,

00:07:57.268 --> 00:08:00.146
and moving toward a top job,

00:08:00.188 --> 00:08:03.733
being an entrepreneur,
being a CEO, gives you power,

00:08:03.774 --> 00:08:06.736
gives you strength,
gives you control,

00:08:06.777 --> 00:08:08.446
the thing they never had
when they were younger,

00:08:08.488 --> 00:08:12.825
so I think that's a driving
force in many entrepreneurs.

00:08:12.867 --> 00:08:16.329
[melancholy music]

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:17.538
[Narrator] Elon Musk attended

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:19.790
Bryanston High
School in Pretoria,

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:21.792
where he thrived academically.

00:08:24.253 --> 00:08:26.506
He had quite a tough upbringing.

00:08:26.547 --> 00:08:29.050
There are some reports
of bullying as well.

00:08:29.091 --> 00:08:33.387
You know, any child that
shows that sort of intellect,

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:37.517
and interest might come under
the glare of the bullies.

00:08:37.558 --> 00:08:40.269
He was even hospitalized
after one particular fight.

00:08:41.437 --> 00:08:42.647
He was a nerd.

00:08:42.688 --> 00:08:44.190
He was bullied at school.

00:08:44.232 --> 00:08:46.734
There was one point he was
even knocked unconscious.

00:08:46.776 --> 00:08:49.070
You know, it wasn't obvious

00:08:49.111 --> 00:08:51.322
that he was a marketing
genius at that point.

00:08:53.282 --> 00:08:55.409
[ref whistle blows]

00:08:55.451 --> 00:08:57.537
Elon took on wrestling classes,

00:08:57.578 --> 00:09:00.039
and a bit of judo in an
attempt to compensate

00:09:00.081 --> 00:09:02.375
with a bit of added physicality,

00:09:02.416 --> 00:09:03.793
and then he grew
up a little bit,

00:09:03.834 --> 00:09:05.461
got a growth spurt,
got a bit taller,

00:09:05.503 --> 00:09:08.756
and really took the
bullies head on.

00:09:08.798 --> 00:09:11.092
[suspenseful music]

00:09:11.133 --> 00:09:14.053
[Narrator] After spending
three years at Bryanston High,

00:09:14.095 --> 00:09:17.139
Elon attended Pretoria
High School for boys.

00:09:18.307 --> 00:09:22.812
Elon attended Boys High
from 1986 until 1988,

00:09:22.853 --> 00:09:25.439
which is the year
he matriculated.

00:09:25.481 --> 00:09:27.066
He excelled in science,

00:09:27.108 --> 00:09:30.236
and he also excelled
in computer studies.

00:09:30.278 --> 00:09:33.364
He, in fact, got a
distinction for both science

00:09:33.406 --> 00:09:35.658
and computer studies in 1988.

00:09:36.742 --> 00:09:38.035
As I do recall his grades,

00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:39.912
'cause I have them
in my mark book,

00:09:39.954 --> 00:09:43.541
and he was definitely
top of the science class.

00:09:43.583 --> 00:09:45.793
I've taught many
boys over the years,

00:09:45.835 --> 00:09:48.296
and Elon did stick in my mind.

00:09:48.337 --> 00:09:50.464
You know, I can't tell
you where every boy sat,

00:09:50.506 --> 00:09:52.133
but I can remember Elon

00:09:52.174 --> 00:09:53.759
sitting at the back
of the classroom

00:09:53.801 --> 00:09:55.303
getting on with his work.

00:09:55.344 --> 00:09:56.554
He wasn't one of the boys

00:09:56.596 --> 00:09:58.973
that wanted attention
all the time.

00:09:59.015 --> 00:10:01.350
We always take a lot of
pride in all of the boys,

00:10:01.392 --> 00:10:02.893
but from a personal
point of view

00:10:02.935 --> 00:10:05.187
having taught Elon science,

00:10:05.229 --> 00:10:07.982
I'd like to think that
I made a small input

00:10:08.024 --> 00:10:09.650
in his formative years.

00:10:09.692 --> 00:10:12.069
I've read would
like to go to Mars.

00:10:12.111 --> 00:10:14.614
I'm hoping he'll do
that a lot sooner,

00:10:14.655 --> 00:10:17.617
but Star Trek style so
he can invite me there.

00:10:18.492 --> 00:10:20.494
[upbeat music]

00:10:20.536 --> 00:10:23.164
[Narrator] Elon Musk
was always different.

00:10:23.205 --> 00:10:25.916
His entrepreneurial
ventures started early.

00:10:27.835 --> 00:10:31.964
In 1984, a science fiction
inspired tech success

00:10:32.006 --> 00:10:33.883
gave a hint of his future

00:10:33.924 --> 00:10:36.344
when he sold his first
commercial software.

00:10:37.845 --> 00:10:40.473
[Jeff] He started working on
computers at the age of 10,

00:10:40.514 --> 00:10:42.850
working very hard,
and being very clever

00:10:42.892 --> 00:10:44.894
in developing programs.

00:10:44.935 --> 00:10:47.355
He develops a video
game called Blastar.

00:10:47.396 --> 00:10:51.233
He famously sold this to
a PC magazine for $500.

00:10:51.275 --> 00:10:53.319
Elon was asked about

00:10:53.361 --> 00:10:55.363
what sets him apart
from other people,

00:10:55.404 --> 00:10:56.864
and he himself said,

00:10:56.906 --> 00:11:01.077
there were things that were
very obvious to him growing up

00:11:01.118 --> 00:11:02.995
that other people
didn't understand,

00:11:03.037 --> 00:11:04.372
how conduction works,

00:11:04.413 --> 00:11:08.000
how light and wires make
a light switch go on,

00:11:08.042 --> 00:11:11.379
things that to him were
immediately clear and obvious,

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:13.589
whereas other people
didn't automatically,

00:11:13.631 --> 00:11:15.383
especially when they're like 14,

00:11:15.424 --> 00:11:17.176
understand what was going on.

00:11:17.218 --> 00:11:18.678
He played a lot of chess,

00:11:18.719 --> 00:11:21.013
and as a result of that was
always sort of thinking ahead.

00:11:21.055 --> 00:11:23.307
In chess you play to
think moves ahead.

00:11:23.349 --> 00:11:25.184
So, even when he was younger,

00:11:25.226 --> 00:11:27.603
he was looking at ideas for

00:11:27.645 --> 00:11:30.064
what might make a good
business in the short term,

00:11:30.106 --> 00:11:33.275
and then what could be part
of a grand plan further on.

00:11:33.317 --> 00:11:36.028
[dramatic music]

00:11:38.989 --> 00:11:40.908
[Narrator] After graduating
from high school,

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:44.453
Musk briefly attended the
University of Pretoria

00:11:44.495 --> 00:11:47.873
before deciding to
leave South Africa.

00:11:47.915 --> 00:11:50.459
The story of Elon Musk's
wealth and his rise

00:11:50.501 --> 00:11:53.379
is tied up in part with
the American dream.

00:11:53.421 --> 00:11:54.797
It was always ingrained to him

00:11:54.839 --> 00:11:56.924
that South Africa wasn't
where he was going to achieve

00:11:56.966 --> 00:11:57.842
the ambitions he had.

00:11:57.883 --> 00:11:59.385
He wanted to be in America.

00:11:59.427 --> 00:12:01.345
He wanted to be a
part of that scene.

00:12:01.387 --> 00:12:03.264
Initially he couldn't
go directly to America.

00:12:03.305 --> 00:12:05.224
He actually had to
go through Canada.

00:12:05.266 --> 00:12:08.018
[dramatic music]

00:12:09.186 --> 00:12:11.230
[Narrator] Elon spent
two years studying

00:12:11.272 --> 00:12:13.733
at Queen's University
in Ontario.

00:12:15.109 --> 00:12:17.653
He funded his own degrees
in Canada and the US

00:12:17.695 --> 00:12:20.448
by getting scholarships
and working part time,

00:12:20.489 --> 00:12:23.451
so he didn't have a huge
amount of money behind him.

00:12:23.492 --> 00:12:26.328
He has a famous university
paper where he talks about

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:28.456
wanting to conquer different
areas of the world,

00:12:28.497 --> 00:12:30.666
and those included the
internet, space exploration,

00:12:30.708 --> 00:12:32.752
solar energy,
artificial intelligence,

00:12:32.793 --> 00:12:34.670
and brain learning
interfaces as well.

00:12:35.588 --> 00:12:38.340
[ethereal music]

00:12:39.842 --> 00:12:42.928
Elon Musk married his
childhood sweetheart.

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:44.513
They met at university.

00:12:44.555 --> 00:12:47.349
Her name was Justine and she
was absolutely beautiful,

00:12:47.391 --> 00:12:48.642
and on their first date

00:12:48.684 --> 00:12:50.227
they were supposed to
go out for ice cream.

00:12:50.269 --> 00:12:53.731
She actually said yes, and
then basically ditched him,

00:12:53.773 --> 00:12:56.358
and went off to do
some studying instead.

00:12:57.318 --> 00:12:58.903
He didn't take no for an answer.

00:12:58.944 --> 00:12:59.862
He pursued her.

00:12:59.904 --> 00:13:01.739
He continued to pursue her,

00:13:01.781 --> 00:13:03.574
and even after Elon left

00:13:03.616 --> 00:13:05.993
the university he
attended with Justine

00:13:06.035 --> 00:13:07.453
and moved to Horton
Business School,

00:13:07.495 --> 00:13:10.581
he still sent her
roses regularly.

00:13:10.623 --> 00:13:13.083
When Elon Musk is
interested in you,

00:13:13.125 --> 00:13:15.252
I think he comes on
like a steam train

00:13:15.294 --> 00:13:18.005
or an electric
steam train perhaps,

00:13:18.047 --> 00:13:20.007
but Elon Musk is unstoppable.

00:13:20.049 --> 00:13:21.634
He sees what he wants,

00:13:21.675 --> 00:13:25.012
and he does everything
he needs to do to get it.

00:13:25.054 --> 00:13:28.557
He's like that in business
and he's like that in love.

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:36.190
[Narrator] Having
completed degrees

00:13:36.232 --> 00:13:37.983
in business and physics,

00:13:38.025 --> 00:13:42.530
in 1995 Musk abandoned
the PhD at Stanford

00:13:42.571 --> 00:13:44.573
and headed for Silicon Valley.

00:13:47.493 --> 00:13:49.203
He famously dropped
out of his third degree

00:13:49.245 --> 00:13:51.872
at Stanford University in
California after just two days.

00:13:51.914 --> 00:13:53.082
And, he had launched a company

00:13:53.123 --> 00:13:55.501
with his brother
Kimball called Zip2,

00:13:55.543 --> 00:13:58.504
and it was effectively a
form of online Yellow Pages

00:13:58.546 --> 00:14:00.422
that he sold to
newspapers at the time.

00:14:00.464 --> 00:14:03.384
And, he struck lucky when
the company was bought.

00:14:03.425 --> 00:14:06.011
They actually ended up
selling it to Compaq,

00:14:06.053 --> 00:14:08.722
for about $307 million,

00:14:08.764 --> 00:14:10.558
when he was only 27 years old.

00:14:10.599 --> 00:14:13.602
He made about $22
million back then,

00:14:13.644 --> 00:14:15.229
which is wonderful,
an awful lot of money,

00:14:15.271 --> 00:14:16.730
but in typical Elon style,

00:14:16.772 --> 00:14:21.235
he recycled half of that
into his next venture,

00:14:21.277 --> 00:14:24.697
and which eventually
ended up becoming PayPal.

00:14:24.738 --> 00:14:26.365
[suspenseful music]

00:14:26.407 --> 00:14:27.741
[Narrator] Having disrupted

00:14:27.783 --> 00:14:31.370
the traditional paper
advertising industry with Zip2,

00:14:31.412 --> 00:14:36.125
Musk's next venture took on
the global financial market.

00:14:36.166 --> 00:14:37.710
So, he worked to setup a company

00:14:37.751 --> 00:14:38.961
called X.com,

00:14:39.003 --> 00:14:41.630
which is the precursor
to what is now PayPal.

00:14:41.672 --> 00:14:43.048
X.com was supposed to be

00:14:43.090 --> 00:14:46.468
a full services financial
services business.

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:49.096
So, it sounds sort
of archaic now,

00:14:49.138 --> 00:14:50.723
but back at the time
putting together

00:14:50.764 --> 00:14:53.517
a financial services
suite on the internet

00:14:53.559 --> 00:14:56.020
was a rather revolutionary idea.

00:14:56.061 --> 00:14:58.606
He had one internet
win under his belt.

00:14:58.647 --> 00:15:01.317
So, it was not
especially challenging

00:15:01.358 --> 00:15:04.987
to bring on new engineers,
or new business people

00:15:05.029 --> 00:15:06.238
who wanted to work on something

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:07.656
that seemed very exciting

00:15:07.698 --> 00:15:10.409
with someone who had
already won already once.

00:15:10.451 --> 00:15:13.203
So, that was a big
feather in his cap.

00:15:13.245 --> 00:15:15.039
It was, I think,
more challenging

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:20.085
to persuade outside investors
that it was a good idea,

00:15:21.253 --> 00:15:22.671
'cause they were not
swayed by one win.

00:15:22.713 --> 00:15:25.799
It was not enough to buy public
opinion in Silicon Valley.

00:15:25.841 --> 00:15:27.343
The jury was still sort of out

00:15:27.384 --> 00:15:31.013
on whether he was genius
or crazy or just a big ego,

00:15:31.055 --> 00:15:32.806
or how it was all
gonna play out,

00:15:32.848 --> 00:15:35.309
but he invested
$10 or $12 million

00:15:35.351 --> 00:15:37.061
of his own money into X.com,

00:15:37.102 --> 00:15:39.647
so we didn't need a whole lot
of money right off the bat.

00:15:39.688 --> 00:15:41.482
He sort of took
care of all of that.

00:15:42.942 --> 00:15:44.652
[Narrator] Musk
was revolutionary

00:15:44.693 --> 00:15:46.946
in his strategy at X.com,

00:15:46.987 --> 00:15:49.698
gifting new customers
cash rewards,

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:52.743
and creating peer-to-peer
payment systems.

00:15:52.785 --> 00:15:55.329
My initial impressions
were just that he was

00:15:55.371 --> 00:15:57.581
very focused and very passionate

00:15:57.623 --> 00:16:00.334
on what he was about to do.

00:16:00.376 --> 00:16:02.544
It seemed very clear early on

00:16:02.586 --> 00:16:06.048
that Elon was not playing by
the standard book of life,

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:08.175
or business, or anything else.

00:16:08.217 --> 00:16:12.137
So, I was just gonna
be along for the ride.

00:16:13.555 --> 00:16:16.058
At the beginning Elon
wasn't easy to read at all,

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:18.310
mostly because you
just had no idea

00:16:18.352 --> 00:16:20.312
how he was going to
react to anything.

00:16:20.354 --> 00:16:22.189
I had no experience with someone

00:16:22.231 --> 00:16:24.024
who thought the way that he did,

00:16:24.066 --> 00:16:25.526
or talked the way that he did,

00:16:25.567 --> 00:16:27.861
or had as much confidence
about what he was doing,

00:16:27.903 --> 00:16:29.405
and in himself as he did.

00:16:29.446 --> 00:16:34.368
It was so foreign to me as
someone who grew up in Iowa,

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:36.120
or maybe it's
foreign to everyone.

00:16:36.161 --> 00:16:37.746
I don't know.

00:16:37.788 --> 00:16:41.041
[dramatic music]

00:16:41.083 --> 00:16:42.376
[Narrator] PayPal was voted

00:16:42.418 --> 00:16:46.714
one of the 10 worst
business ideas in 1999.

00:16:46.755 --> 00:16:48.841
Elon Musk was instrumental

00:16:48.882 --> 00:16:52.052
in the company's focus on
a global payment system,

00:16:52.094 --> 00:16:55.222
which would change the
course of the internet.

00:16:55.264 --> 00:16:56.932
People probably know
him mostly from PayPal.

00:16:56.974 --> 00:17:00.060
That's when he really first
came to public consciousness.

00:17:00.102 --> 00:17:03.063
PayPal was how are we
going to turn finances

00:17:03.105 --> 00:17:05.607
into an online transaction?

00:17:05.649 --> 00:17:06.608
How are we gonna make it easy?

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:07.860
How are we gonna make
it more efficient?

00:17:07.901 --> 00:17:09.319
It sounds sort of obvious now,

00:17:09.361 --> 00:17:12.406
but back then people were still
afraid to buy books online,

00:17:12.448 --> 00:17:15.951
so asking them to put all their
financial data in one place

00:17:15.993 --> 00:17:18.245
was not a solid proposition.

00:17:18.287 --> 00:17:20.581
The goal of the company
that we were building

00:17:20.622 --> 00:17:22.875
was always trying
to do something

00:17:22.916 --> 00:17:24.710
very positive for humanity,

00:17:24.752 --> 00:17:27.963
and he was thinking about
space, and solar panels,

00:17:28.005 --> 00:17:29.465
and all of that way back then.

00:17:29.506 --> 00:17:32.926
So, we knew that this was
sort of a stepping-stone

00:17:32.968 --> 00:17:34.803
on the way to grander dreams.

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:37.931
[Narrator] PayPal soon became

00:17:37.973 --> 00:17:40.684
the online payment
system of choice,

00:17:40.726 --> 00:17:42.478
from a few thousand users,

00:17:42.519 --> 00:17:45.939
multiplying to more than
a million within months.

00:17:47.107 --> 00:17:48.776
The person to person
payment side of things

00:17:48.817 --> 00:17:52.488
took off so quickly, we were
gaining traction like crazy,

00:17:52.529 --> 00:17:55.282
and then the online auction
market took hold of it,

00:17:55.324 --> 00:17:57.910
and it blew up into
this huge thing.

00:17:57.951 --> 00:17:59.620
We were adding tens of thousands

00:17:59.661 --> 00:18:00.954
of new accounts every day,

00:18:00.996 --> 00:18:03.832
which at the time was
fairly unprecedented.

00:18:05.292 --> 00:18:08.170
Elon was very hands-on in
all aspects of the company.

00:18:08.212 --> 00:18:11.173
It was his money after all
that we were all playing with.

00:18:11.215 --> 00:18:14.635
So, I think when it's your
money on the table, you have,

00:18:14.676 --> 00:18:15.969
it's in your best interests

00:18:16.011 --> 00:18:18.597
to be very involved
in all the details.

00:18:18.639 --> 00:18:20.808
[car honks]

00:18:20.849 --> 00:18:23.560
Everyday was a new
sort of fire, right?

00:18:23.602 --> 00:18:25.646
Everyday something
was burning down

00:18:25.687 --> 00:18:27.231
that needed to be addressed.

00:18:27.272 --> 00:18:29.483
We had a customer
service nightmare

00:18:29.525 --> 00:18:31.193
where soon after
launching the service,

00:18:31.235 --> 00:18:33.862
we had thousands and thousands
of emails in the queue,

00:18:33.904 --> 00:18:36.657
because people
didn't understand,

00:18:36.698 --> 00:18:38.242
again, the early
days of the internet,

00:18:38.283 --> 00:18:41.078
they didn't understand how
to get their money out.

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:43.705
And so, they would
freak out, and call in,

00:18:43.747 --> 00:18:45.541
or email like 12
different times.

00:18:45.582 --> 00:18:47.751
So, we have two million
emails in the cue

00:18:47.793 --> 00:18:50.003
and we only had 10
customer service people

00:18:50.045 --> 00:18:51.839
dedicated to dealing with that.

00:18:51.880 --> 00:18:54.258
And so, we really had no idea
what we were going to do.

00:18:54.299 --> 00:18:57.803
Customer service was enormously
expensive in California.

00:18:57.845 --> 00:19:00.305
And so, I said you know
this is gonna sound crazy,

00:19:00.347 --> 00:19:03.183
but I have a huge extended
family in the Midwest.

00:19:03.225 --> 00:19:06.520
I could train them all to
do customer service at home.

00:19:06.562 --> 00:19:09.189
Elon laughed at first, and
then a couple of hours later

00:19:09.231 --> 00:19:11.692
came back and said all
right, get on a plane.

00:19:11.733 --> 00:19:14.444
So, I went out and taught
10 of my family members

00:19:14.486 --> 00:19:16.029
how to do customer
service from home,

00:19:16.071 --> 00:19:18.323
and then my sister
ended up hiring

00:19:18.365 --> 00:19:21.869
12 stay at home parents in
a small town in Nebraska

00:19:21.910 --> 00:19:23.453
who started all
working from home.

00:19:23.495 --> 00:19:25.455
And they were so good
that we ended up building

00:19:25.497 --> 00:19:27.916
a customer service center
in Omaha, Nebraska,

00:19:27.958 --> 00:19:30.711
which now employs 1800 people,

00:19:30.752 --> 00:19:32.337
and that turned out fantastic.

00:19:32.379 --> 00:19:35.048
We went from zero to a
hundred people in six weeks

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:36.550
out in Omaha,

00:19:36.592 --> 00:19:39.970
and that was probably the
most challenging thing,

00:19:40.012 --> 00:19:42.890
but we were a little
short of options, so.

00:19:42.931 --> 00:19:45.017
[laughs]

00:19:45.058 --> 00:19:46.476
[dramatic music]

00:19:46.518 --> 00:19:49.104
[Narrator] Led by his
passion for perfectionism,

00:19:49.146 --> 00:19:52.816
determination, and
a heroic work ethic,

00:19:52.858 --> 00:19:54.902
Musk has succeeded in markets

00:19:54.943 --> 00:19:58.113
that were previously considered
impossible to conquer.

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:04.161
[Julie] He's always had higher
expectations for himself

00:20:04.203 --> 00:20:05.454
than he did for other people,

00:20:05.495 --> 00:20:09.583
so he pushed himself
very, very hard.

00:20:09.625 --> 00:20:10.959
There's a lot of pressure on him

00:20:11.001 --> 00:20:12.753
because he has a
lot of expectations,

00:20:12.794 --> 00:20:14.463
a lot of responsibility

00:20:14.504 --> 00:20:18.342
to his shareholders, to the
capital that has sourced,

00:20:18.383 --> 00:20:20.677
to his staff, his colleagues,

00:20:20.719 --> 00:20:22.262
so there's a lot of demands.

00:20:22.304 --> 00:20:24.598
So, you can't just
say you know what?

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:25.849
This is all good and great.

00:20:25.891 --> 00:20:27.768
I'm just gonna take
six months off.

00:20:27.809 --> 00:20:30.187
Why, because I've got 20
billion, so it's okay.

00:20:31.104 --> 00:20:32.522
No, it doesn't work like that.

00:20:33.941 --> 00:20:35.234
As the stakes get higher,

00:20:35.275 --> 00:20:36.860
and the more people
that get involved

00:20:36.902 --> 00:20:38.820
there's more pressure
on that front,

00:20:38.862 --> 00:20:43.367
but I think he looks
at it, and turns away.

00:20:44.826 --> 00:20:48.372
What drives Elon, what
pushes him, I don't get it,

00:20:48.413 --> 00:20:50.916
but he's making
changes in the world,

00:20:50.958 --> 00:20:53.502
because he doesn't
ever stop and rest.

00:20:53.543 --> 00:20:56.129
He just keeps going.

00:20:56.171 --> 00:21:00.133
Elon Musk is famous for his
work ethic, which is nonstop.

00:21:00.175 --> 00:21:03.637
I think he has two modes,
and there's stop and go.

00:21:03.679 --> 00:21:05.264
He works around the clock

00:21:05.305 --> 00:21:07.057
and he expects everyone
else to do the same.

00:21:07.099 --> 00:21:10.310
Elon was up all hours
of the night working.

00:21:10.352 --> 00:21:13.480
He would send emails at
midnight or 1:00 or 2:00 a.m.

00:21:13.522 --> 00:21:15.565
and just be very
focused on some problem

00:21:15.607 --> 00:21:17.067
that we were trying to solve.

00:21:17.109 --> 00:21:19.194
If you hear stories from
SpaceX in the early days,

00:21:19.236 --> 00:21:21.446
people were really struggling
to keep pace with him,

00:21:21.488 --> 00:21:22.906
but that's how he works,

00:21:22.948 --> 00:21:25.033
and if you can't keep
pace, you're out the door.

00:21:26.868 --> 00:21:28.078
I thought he was a good boss.

00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:29.746
It's sort of infamous now

00:21:29.788 --> 00:21:32.416
that he runs through
PR people very quickly,

00:21:32.457 --> 00:21:35.669
but at that time, I thought
I was just gonna be like,

00:21:35.711 --> 00:21:37.170
a normal PR person. [laughs]

00:21:37.212 --> 00:21:41.008
And so, Elon wasn't really
gonna have any of that.

00:21:41.049 --> 00:21:43.051
He expected a lot of his people,

00:21:43.093 --> 00:21:45.220
but we all expected
a lot of each other,

00:21:45.262 --> 00:21:46.888
and that's how you get it done.

00:21:47.889 --> 00:21:51.685
In the culture of startups
the expectation is

00:21:51.727 --> 00:21:53.687
that everybody is
working all the time.

00:21:53.729 --> 00:21:55.689
You get there at 9:00
or 10:00 in the morning

00:21:55.731 --> 00:21:57.691
and not leave
until 9:00 or 10:00

00:21:57.733 --> 00:21:59.401
or maybe after that at night.

00:21:59.443 --> 00:22:02.779
And Elon was definitely working
until midnight every night.

00:22:02.821 --> 00:22:05.949
That was just what we
did, and it was exciting.

00:22:05.991 --> 00:22:08.535
Rarely did anyone complain
about that kind of thing,

00:22:08.577 --> 00:22:11.538
'cause you really felt like
you were changing the world.

00:22:11.580 --> 00:22:14.374
[dramatic music]

00:22:15.625 --> 00:22:17.127
[Narrator] By the year 2000,

00:22:17.169 --> 00:22:20.005
Musk had married Justine Wilson.

00:22:20.047 --> 00:22:22.549
He was always
pining for Justine,

00:22:22.591 --> 00:22:24.676
and he was still in
Canada at that point.

00:22:24.718 --> 00:22:27.054
And so, it was lovely
when she came down.

00:22:27.095 --> 00:22:30.390
The couple did suffer a
tragedy very early on.

00:22:30.432 --> 00:22:33.560
Their baby boy was
10 weeks old, Nevada,

00:22:33.602 --> 00:22:36.063
when he died suddenly of SIDS.

00:22:36.104 --> 00:22:39.399
It was beyond difficult for
both of them to cope with.

00:22:39.441 --> 00:22:42.569
Justine immediately sought
IVF, and fertility help,

00:22:42.611 --> 00:22:46.114
and went on to have five boys,

00:22:46.156 --> 00:22:49.701
one set of triplets and
one set of twin boys.

00:22:49.743 --> 00:22:52.204
So, very quickly they went from

00:22:52.245 --> 00:22:54.164
having no children
to having five.

00:22:54.206 --> 00:22:56.333
Justine has written
extensively about

00:22:56.375 --> 00:22:58.668
how it caused a strain
in the marriage,

00:22:58.710 --> 00:23:00.337
as it might do, as it would do,

00:23:00.379 --> 00:23:04.758
but this tragic event of
losing their infant son

00:23:04.800 --> 00:23:07.594
unfortunately didn't
draw the couple closer,

00:23:07.636 --> 00:23:09.429
but rather was the
beginning, perhaps,

00:23:09.471 --> 00:23:11.807
of what ultimately
would pull them apart.

00:23:14.309 --> 00:23:15.936
[Narrator] As PayPal flourished,

00:23:15.977 --> 00:23:18.021
boardroom troubles dogged Musk.

00:23:19.189 --> 00:23:21.316
An internal disagreement
with the Board

00:23:21.358 --> 00:23:25.529
resulted in Elon being
ousted from his own company.

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:26.905
There was a time
when Peter Theil

00:23:26.947 --> 00:23:30.075
and Elon were co-CEOs at PayPal,

00:23:30.117 --> 00:23:32.619
but there were certain
differences in opinion

00:23:32.661 --> 00:23:35.247
about the technology and
the branding of the company

00:23:35.288 --> 00:23:39.126
that led to the vote of no
confidence to oust Elon.

00:23:39.167 --> 00:23:43.797
And Elon unfortunately,
was in South Africa.

00:23:43.839 --> 00:23:46.466
I had just spoken with
him on the phone that day,

00:23:46.508 --> 00:23:47.801
and he's like how bad is it?

00:23:47.843 --> 00:23:49.469
I'm like oh, it's not that bad.

00:23:50.887 --> 00:23:53.098
And then that night I went
home, and I was laying in bed,

00:23:53.140 --> 00:23:55.100
and I started realizing and
started putting together

00:23:55.142 --> 00:23:58.311
all the pieces of who I
had seen in the office,

00:23:58.353 --> 00:23:59.813
and what was happening,

00:23:59.855 --> 00:24:02.649
and at about 10:30 at night
I sat straight up in bed,

00:24:02.691 --> 00:24:05.735
and I'm sure I said some
swear word at the ceiling.

00:24:05.777 --> 00:24:07.446
I got up, and I went
back in the office,

00:24:07.487 --> 00:24:09.448
and sure enough
everybody was there,

00:24:09.489 --> 00:24:11.533
and that was when
they were doing it.

00:24:11.575 --> 00:24:13.452
They were talking to
the Board on the phone,

00:24:13.493 --> 00:24:15.704
and ousting him, and I
tried to call to Elon,

00:24:15.745 --> 00:24:17.873
and he was on a plane,
and I couldn't get him.

00:24:17.914 --> 00:24:19.749
I couldn't get him
until the morning,

00:24:19.791 --> 00:24:22.210
and by then it was too
late, it was already done.

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:26.923
I'm sure there was quite a
bit of unhappiness early on,

00:24:26.965 --> 00:24:29.384
but he really handled
it marvelously.

00:24:30.719 --> 00:24:33.722
[suspenseful music]

00:24:38.393 --> 00:24:40.270
[Narrator] Musk left PayPal,

00:24:40.312 --> 00:24:42.189
but as the largest shareholder,

00:24:42.230 --> 00:24:45.066
remained in an advisory role.

00:24:45.108 --> 00:24:48.320
His next windfall
arrived next 2002,

00:24:48.361 --> 00:24:50.697
when eBay bought PayPal,

00:24:50.739 --> 00:24:53.533
leaving him wealthy
beyond belief.

00:24:54.534 --> 00:24:56.411
That was where Elon

00:24:56.453 --> 00:24:58.830
started to make
some serious money.

00:24:58.872 --> 00:25:02.751
He cashed in about
$165 million wonderful.

00:25:02.792 --> 00:25:04.169
What was amazing is he
plowed almost all of it

00:25:04.211 --> 00:25:06.546
into these new
business ventures.

00:25:06.588 --> 00:25:08.048
He didn't put any
aside for himself,

00:25:08.089 --> 00:25:11.134
didn't think of sort
of personal gain,

00:25:11.176 --> 00:25:12.719
in short term at least.

00:25:12.761 --> 00:25:14.513
A lot of us would've
just retired.

00:25:14.554 --> 00:25:17.307
He made the money in PayPal,
he could've just stopped,

00:25:17.349 --> 00:25:19.601
and you know, bought a place
in the south of France,

00:25:19.643 --> 00:25:22.687
and said I'm retiring,
I'm 28, and be done,

00:25:22.729 --> 00:25:23.897
but he ended up starting

00:25:23.939 --> 00:25:26.816
three other billion
dollar companies.

00:25:26.858 --> 00:25:29.611
[dramatic music]

00:25:31.071 --> 00:25:33.240
- He took $100 million
and put it into SpaceX,

00:25:33.281 --> 00:25:35.450
$70 million ended
up going to Tesla,

00:25:35.492 --> 00:25:37.953
and about $10 million
going to SolarCity.

00:25:37.994 --> 00:25:39.996
Most people would
say $165 million,

00:25:40.038 --> 00:25:41.248
I'm set for life.

00:25:41.289 --> 00:25:42.791
That's it, where's the beach?

00:25:42.832 --> 00:25:44.125
Let's go.

00:25:44.167 --> 00:25:46.628
Not Elon, and I have a
lot of respect for that.

00:25:47.712 --> 00:25:49.130
[Emcee] Ladies and gentleman,

00:25:49.172 --> 00:25:50.799
-please welcome Elon Musk.
-[audience applauding]

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:52.300
[Narrator] In 2001,

00:25:52.342 --> 00:25:54.886
Elon established
the Musk Foundation,

00:25:54.928 --> 00:25:57.889
delivering solar energy
systems to areas of the world

00:25:57.931 --> 00:26:00.517
struck by natural disasters.

00:26:00.559 --> 00:26:02.143
He also pledged to give away

00:26:02.185 --> 00:26:05.730
at least half of his
wealth to philanthropy.

00:26:05.772 --> 00:26:07.440
A lot of people think

00:26:07.482 --> 00:26:09.734
that entrepreneurs
are driven by money,

00:26:09.776 --> 00:26:11.528
and that's why they're
serial entrepreneurs.

00:26:11.570 --> 00:26:13.238
They wanna make more and more.

00:26:13.280 --> 00:26:15.073
Really after you
make $50 million,

00:26:15.115 --> 00:26:16.449
you need to make more money?

00:26:16.491 --> 00:26:19.119
Not really, that's
not a good driver.

00:26:19.160 --> 00:26:21.329
They are not driven
by money at all.

00:26:22.330 --> 00:26:24.040
He's aware of it no doubt.

00:26:24.082 --> 00:26:25.917
If you've got $20
billion floating around,

00:26:25.959 --> 00:26:27.252
I'm sure you would've noticed,

00:26:27.294 --> 00:26:29.462
but I don't think that's
the main driver for him.

00:26:29.504 --> 00:26:30.505
I really don't.

00:26:30.547 --> 00:26:32.299
I think he's got
a bigger vision,

00:26:32.340 --> 00:26:35.468
beyond this little
thing called money,

00:26:35.510 --> 00:26:39.055
but money helps him
execute the dream,

00:26:39.097 --> 00:26:41.182
so it is a necessary thing.

00:26:42.559 --> 00:26:45.312
It's certainly not the pursuit
of money at this point.

00:26:45.353 --> 00:26:47.314
I think he realized we're here,

00:26:47.355 --> 00:26:49.399
and it's more
interesting to spend time

00:26:49.441 --> 00:26:53.028
doing something that you find
meaningful in your heart,

00:26:53.069 --> 00:26:54.404
and that's what he's doing.

00:26:54.446 --> 00:26:55.905
It's almost like a drug.

00:26:55.947 --> 00:26:57.574
When you've done one
successful thing,

00:26:57.616 --> 00:26:59.075
you wanna the other one,

00:26:59.117 --> 00:27:00.994
you wanna come up with a new
vision and keep learning.

00:27:01.036 --> 00:27:03.121
It's just a way of being.

00:27:03.163 --> 00:27:04.706
He would be bored on the beach.

00:27:06.082 --> 00:27:09.002
[Julie] It has to be a very
big world changing goal

00:27:09.044 --> 00:27:10.295
for him to get up
in the morning.

00:27:10.337 --> 00:27:12.297
That's just the way he's wired.

00:27:12.339 --> 00:27:14.841
So, I don't expect that to stop.

00:27:14.883 --> 00:27:16.259
I think it actually
is gonna make

00:27:16.301 --> 00:27:17.510
a difference to the world

00:27:17.552 --> 00:27:19.596
if we transition to
sustainable transport

00:27:19.638 --> 00:27:20.889
sooner rather than later.

00:27:22.515 --> 00:27:25.143
We're not doing this because
we thought it was like

00:27:25.185 --> 00:27:27.395
a way to get rich or anything.

00:27:27.437 --> 00:27:28.855
Like I said, I thought maybe

00:27:28.897 --> 00:27:31.566
a 10 percent chance of
success at the beginning.

00:27:31.608 --> 00:27:34.444
I thought I would just lose
all my money, and I almost did.

00:27:34.486 --> 00:27:35.737
So, it's not like it was,

00:27:36.905 --> 00:27:38.865
I came very close to
losing everything.

00:27:38.907 --> 00:27:41.451
[suspenseful music]

00:27:41.493 --> 00:27:44.371
[Operations] Falcon 9
has cleared the tower.

00:27:44.412 --> 00:27:46.539
[Narrator] Having
conquered cyber space,

00:27:46.581 --> 00:27:50.877
Musk's next mission would
focus on outer space.

00:27:50.919 --> 00:27:54.631
In 2002, he launched one
of the most revolutionary

00:27:54.673 --> 00:27:56.216
businesses of our time,

00:27:56.257 --> 00:27:59.177
focused on shaking up
the space industry.

00:28:01.638 --> 00:28:04.099
The pivotal breakthrough
that's necessary

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:05.892
to make life multi-planetary is

00:28:05.934 --> 00:28:09.229
a fully and rapidly
reusable orbit-class rocket.

00:28:09.270 --> 00:28:10.897
This is a very
difficult thing to do,

00:28:10.939 --> 00:28:12.273
because we live in a planet,

00:28:12.315 --> 00:28:15.318
where that is just
barely possible.

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:16.736
In the last 12 months or so,

00:28:16.778 --> 00:28:19.572
I've come to the conclusion
that it can be solved,

00:28:19.614 --> 00:28:23.118
and I think SpaceX is
going to try to do it.

00:28:23.159 --> 00:28:24.369
We could fail.

00:28:24.411 --> 00:28:27.038
I'm not saying we are
certain of success here,

00:28:27.080 --> 00:28:28.748
but we are going
to try to do it.

00:28:28.790 --> 00:28:30.750
He had already been
talking about energy

00:28:30.792 --> 00:28:32.752
and transportation and space.

00:28:32.794 --> 00:28:34.337
I was like why space?

00:28:34.379 --> 00:28:36.214
He's like space is cool.

00:28:36.256 --> 00:28:38.049
Alright. [laughs]

00:28:38.091 --> 00:28:39.926
No one thought he
could do what he's done

00:28:39.968 --> 00:28:41.886
when he first set out
to achieve his goals.

00:28:41.928 --> 00:28:44.472
I mean this random South African

00:28:44.514 --> 00:28:45.724
coming along in the 2000s

00:28:45.765 --> 00:28:46.975
and saying he wants
to build a rocket

00:28:47.016 --> 00:28:49.561
that can land itself
back on the ground,

00:28:49.602 --> 00:28:51.062
can take things to orbit

00:28:51.104 --> 00:28:53.148
for 10 times cheaper
than anyone else.

00:28:53.189 --> 00:28:55.108
He was laughed out the
room in most places.

00:28:55.150 --> 00:28:56.609
They kind of
ignored him at first

00:28:56.651 --> 00:28:58.319
and then he kind
of put his money

00:28:58.361 --> 00:29:00.113
where his mouth was literally.

00:29:00.155 --> 00:29:01.781
He famously went to Russia once

00:29:01.823 --> 00:29:03.533
to try and buy some
rocket engines off them,

00:29:03.575 --> 00:29:05.785
and he got laughed out
of the country basically

00:29:05.827 --> 00:29:07.287
by their engineers,

00:29:07.328 --> 00:29:10.290
so he came back to the US
and he built his own rocket.

00:29:10.331 --> 00:29:13.835
We were still used to
NASA, space equals NASA,

00:29:13.877 --> 00:29:16.504
but when Elon Musk
started to experiment

00:29:16.546 --> 00:29:17.797
with these new rockets,

00:29:17.839 --> 00:29:19.758
we realized that
Elon Musk and SpaceX

00:29:19.799 --> 00:29:22.844
is a serious space player.

00:29:22.886 --> 00:29:24.262
[Astronaut] Everything
looking good

00:29:24.304 --> 00:29:27.474
for the beginning of
today's space walking tasks.

00:29:28.892 --> 00:29:32.061
[Narrator] The first three
SpaceX launches failed,

00:29:32.103 --> 00:29:35.815
which sparked skepticism
in many investors.

00:29:35.857 --> 00:29:38.067
[Mission Control] The
vehicle on course on track.

00:29:44.783 --> 00:29:49.120
And, we appear to have had
a launch vehicle failure.

00:29:51.206 --> 00:29:52.999
Space flight is a
tricky business.

00:29:53.041 --> 00:29:55.418
I mean, it is
definitely difficult,

00:29:55.460 --> 00:29:57.670
and I think we forget
that sometimes.

00:29:57.712 --> 00:30:01.341
We see launches and
landings as routine.

00:30:01.382 --> 00:30:06.054
It's a huge amount of energy
and dangerous substances,

00:30:06.095 --> 00:30:08.181
and that work is very hard.

00:30:08.223 --> 00:30:10.141
Look where we are today.

00:30:10.183 --> 00:30:12.227
The critics have been silenced.

00:30:13.394 --> 00:30:16.356
[dramatic music]

00:30:16.397 --> 00:30:19.526
[Narrator] Just one year
after founding SpaceX,

00:30:19.567 --> 00:30:22.237
Musk invested in Tesla Motors,

00:30:22.278 --> 00:30:24.197
an electric car manufacturer

00:30:24.239 --> 00:30:28.243
aiming to produce a
power-packed zero polluting car.

00:30:29.661 --> 00:30:31.538
Over the next several years,

00:30:31.579 --> 00:30:34.749
Tesla would take the
lead in a niche market.

00:30:35.875 --> 00:30:37.961
Our goal was to really
to try to create

00:30:38.002 --> 00:30:41.047
the best car in the world
in order to show that

00:30:41.089 --> 00:30:43.716
an electric car can be
the best car in the world.

00:30:45.051 --> 00:30:46.219
Because a lot of people

00:30:46.261 --> 00:30:49.597
have this perception that
electric cars are compromised,

00:30:49.639 --> 00:30:51.391
that there are issues
with electric cars,

00:30:51.432 --> 00:30:52.892
and then if you buy electric,

00:30:52.934 --> 00:30:54.727
it's because you have some
sort of electric fetish.

00:30:54.769 --> 00:30:56.145
What I think is
incredibly important

00:30:56.187 --> 00:30:58.273
is to break that perception
and show that indeed

00:30:58.314 --> 00:31:02.735
the electric car can be
better than any gasoline car.

00:31:02.777 --> 00:31:04.362
Cars were always a big deal.

00:31:04.404 --> 00:31:07.073
He had the F1 McLaren show up

00:31:07.115 --> 00:31:09.993
very early on in the X.com days,

00:31:10.034 --> 00:31:12.537
and there was a Jaguar
very soon after that,

00:31:12.579 --> 00:31:14.247
and he liked them all very much,

00:31:14.289 --> 00:31:16.624
anything that was rare,
expensive, and cool.

00:31:21.796 --> 00:31:23.131
If you look at the revolution

00:31:23.172 --> 00:31:25.758
that's almost upon
us in electric cars,

00:31:25.800 --> 00:31:27.635
Tesla was there first.

00:31:30.013 --> 00:31:31.347
-There's been
various attempts

00:31:31.389 --> 00:31:34.017
to bring in electric
cars, which have failed,

00:31:34.058 --> 00:31:38.313
because big car giants
didn't want them to succeed.

00:31:39.647 --> 00:31:41.441
Elon knew nothing
about electric cars

00:31:41.482 --> 00:31:42.942
when he started out,

00:31:42.984 --> 00:31:44.152
but recognized that
there was a problem there

00:31:44.193 --> 00:31:45.445
that needed to be solved,

00:31:45.486 --> 00:31:47.530
and he just bulldozed
his way through it.

00:31:49.824 --> 00:31:51.659
I'm really excited by
the fact that we've got

00:31:51.701 --> 00:31:55.246
a zero emission sports car
that can go head-to-head

00:31:55.288 --> 00:31:57.123
with a Ferrari and
a Porsche and win.

00:31:58.041 --> 00:32:00.627
This is what's truly disruptive,

00:32:00.668 --> 00:32:03.338
a tech guy coming
into the car space.

00:32:03.379 --> 00:32:05.548
Elon doesn't look
at it as a car.

00:32:05.590 --> 00:32:06.799
It's a piece of software

00:32:06.841 --> 00:32:08.968
with a little bit of
hardware attached to it.

00:32:09.010 --> 00:32:11.054
The driving
experience in a Tesla,

00:32:11.095 --> 00:32:13.306
everybody is raving about it.

00:32:13.348 --> 00:32:15.433
[Test Driver] It was awesome,
it was unbelievable,

00:32:15.475 --> 00:32:17.268
the acceleration was
out of this world.

00:32:17.310 --> 00:32:19.145
It was like a roller coaster.

00:32:19.187 --> 00:32:21.689
I felt like I was
gonna fly or something.

00:32:21.731 --> 00:32:23.858
It's quick, it's silent,

00:32:23.900 --> 00:32:25.985
but also with Teslas
it's the technology,

00:32:26.027 --> 00:32:27.779
the fact that they
update themselves.

00:32:27.820 --> 00:32:30.448
I had a Tesla for a while and
I'll come out in the morning.

00:32:30.490 --> 00:32:32.283
Software update
as your phone does

00:32:32.325 --> 00:32:33.868
and you get stuff on your car

00:32:33.910 --> 00:32:35.995
that you didn't have
the previous night.

00:32:36.037 --> 00:32:40.249
You can actually move your
Tesla out of a parking spot

00:32:40.291 --> 00:32:41.834
with your smartphone.

00:32:41.876 --> 00:32:43.336
So, it's just incredible.

00:32:43.378 --> 00:32:46.089
For somebody who's a
car fan and a tech fan,

00:32:46.130 --> 00:32:48.216
then Tesla do really
tick a lot of boxes.

00:32:51.135 --> 00:32:52.553
The future is electric.

00:32:52.595 --> 00:32:54.847
In the future we won't
be filling up for petrol.

00:32:54.889 --> 00:32:57.892
Elon has understood
that for a decade.

00:32:57.934 --> 00:32:59.602
Already you're seeing
countries across Europe,

00:32:59.644 --> 00:33:01.771
France, the UK,

00:33:01.813 --> 00:33:03.648
who are saying they're gonna
ban the sale of petrol cars

00:33:03.690 --> 00:33:05.233
in the next 10, 20 years,

00:33:05.274 --> 00:33:07.235
and that's pretty
much thanks to Tesla.

00:33:08.027 --> 00:33:10.446
[Elon] In the face of massively
declining oil prices

00:33:10.488 --> 00:33:11.906
I think it actually
becomes more urgent

00:33:11.948 --> 00:33:14.826
that the auto manufactures
transition to electric

00:33:14.867 --> 00:33:16.035
and put a huge amount of effort

00:33:16.077 --> 00:33:18.204
behind their electric
vehicle programs.

00:33:18.246 --> 00:33:21.040
What's amazing is how the
rest of the auto industry

00:33:21.082 --> 00:33:22.792
is sort of following.

00:33:22.834 --> 00:33:24.919
They had this lithium-ion
cell technology

00:33:24.961 --> 00:33:27.338
that other companies
really couldn't match.

00:33:27.380 --> 00:33:29.173
-There isn't a car
company in the world

00:33:29.215 --> 00:33:30.883
who isn't looking at
an electric vehicle.

00:33:30.925 --> 00:33:32.552
There's probably not a
car company in the world

00:33:32.593 --> 00:33:34.262
who hasn't taken a Tesla apart

00:33:34.303 --> 00:33:36.556
to see what's going
on underneath.

00:33:36.597 --> 00:33:38.516
At Tesla we've open
sourced our patents,

00:33:38.558 --> 00:33:40.226
so we're trying to be
as helpful as possible

00:33:40.268 --> 00:33:41.978
for the advent of
electric vehicles

00:33:42.020 --> 00:33:44.605
and we've also said that
our super charge network,

00:33:44.647 --> 00:33:48.151
we're happy to have other
car makers use that network.

00:33:48.192 --> 00:33:50.153
We're really doing
doing everything we can

00:33:50.194 --> 00:33:53.990
to accelerate the advent
of sustainable cars.

00:33:55.908 --> 00:33:58.536
I mean, now we know that
GM and Land Rover have said

00:33:58.578 --> 00:34:00.621
they're gonna start
doing electric cars

00:34:00.663 --> 00:34:03.082
in the near future, 
just electric cars.

00:34:03.124 --> 00:34:06.461
There's so many things
that Tesla has done first,

00:34:06.502 --> 00:34:08.880
which has got every other
car maker around the planet

00:34:08.921 --> 00:34:10.590
looking, thinking yeah,

00:34:10.631 --> 00:34:12.550
we've got to do like
that haven't we?

00:34:12.592 --> 00:34:15.678
The fundamental problem
is the rules today

00:34:15.720 --> 00:34:18.139
incent people to create carbon,

00:34:18.181 --> 00:34:21.642
and this is madness, and
whatever you incent will happen.

00:34:21.684 --> 00:34:25.480
We need to send a clear message
to the negotiating teams

00:34:25.521 --> 00:34:28.066
and to the politicians
that this time

00:34:28.107 --> 00:34:29.942
there needs to be
significant change.

00:34:31.319 --> 00:34:32.403
[Narrator] An essential part

00:34:32.445 --> 00:34:34.864
of Elon Musk's tale of triumph

00:34:34.906 --> 00:34:38.618
is how close he came
to complete disaster.

00:34:38.659 --> 00:34:43.664
By his own admission, 2008 was
the worst year of his life.

00:34:45.083 --> 00:34:47.460
His companies were battered
by the economic meltdown.

00:34:47.502 --> 00:34:51.464
Three failed launches left
SpaceX hanging in the balance,

00:34:51.506 --> 00:34:54.217
and Tesla was on the
verge of bankruptcy.

00:34:55.301 --> 00:34:58.054
Elon was widely
assumed to be finished.

00:35:00.389 --> 00:35:02.350
He certainly has
his ups and downs,

00:35:02.391 --> 00:35:06.562
and believe me it has been
lots and lots of ups and downs.

00:35:06.604 --> 00:35:08.981
He seems to have the
media on his back

00:35:09.023 --> 00:35:10.191
almost permanently,

00:35:10.233 --> 00:35:12.443
but that seems to be the
price of his success.

00:35:12.485 --> 00:35:14.987
In the early years, it really
came down to a knife edge,

00:35:15.029 --> 00:35:17.990
as to whether or not
SpaceX was going to make it

00:35:18.032 --> 00:35:20.952
whether or not it was
going to go bankrupt.

00:35:20.993 --> 00:35:23.329
He was having to allocate
resources and money

00:35:23.371 --> 00:35:26.833
between Tesla and SpaceX in
order to keep them going,

00:35:26.874 --> 00:35:28.960
and it came down to
having to borrow money

00:35:29.001 --> 00:35:30.211
from friends and family,

00:35:30.253 --> 00:35:31.921
and bear in mind this is
a man who made millions

00:35:31.963 --> 00:35:34.340
off a software sale earlier.

00:35:34.382 --> 00:35:36.384
Elon Musk he has had
plenty of failures,

00:35:36.425 --> 00:35:38.094
failed rocket launches,

00:35:38.136 --> 00:35:40.680
and you have to go through
this learning curve.

00:35:40.721 --> 00:35:42.306
[Julie] With the early
rockets and all of that

00:35:42.348 --> 00:35:43.641
he didn't seem to
understand that

00:35:43.683 --> 00:35:45.685
the chances were they
were going to blow up,

00:35:45.726 --> 00:35:47.854
but sometimes through
your greatest failure,

00:35:47.895 --> 00:35:49.730
you make your greatest
steps forward.

00:35:50.898 --> 00:35:51.816
He just keeps going,

00:35:51.858 --> 00:35:53.526
he keeps pushing the boat out.

00:35:53.568 --> 00:35:56.070
It is just an
insatiable appetite

00:35:56.112 --> 00:35:57.989
to just keep going
whatever happens.

00:35:58.030 --> 00:35:59.740
Yes is yes,

00:36:00.700 --> 00:36:02.076
maybe is yes,

00:36:02.118 --> 00:36:04.829
and a no is just a delayed yes.

00:36:04.871 --> 00:36:06.581
There were a million
places along the way

00:36:06.622 --> 00:36:08.624
where he could have
stopped or been derailed

00:36:08.666 --> 00:36:11.169
or decided to go buy that
island and just sit on it,

00:36:11.210 --> 00:36:12.128
but he didn't.

00:36:13.421 --> 00:36:15.715
They're risk takers,
they love it.

00:36:15.756 --> 00:36:17.884
They live to be
on the knife edge.

00:36:17.925 --> 00:36:20.052
Failure is expected.

00:36:20.094 --> 00:36:22.305
Entrepreneurs don't
expect necessarily

00:36:22.346 --> 00:36:23.806
to succeed all the time,

00:36:23.848 --> 00:36:26.517
which is a characteristic
that makes them successful,

00:36:26.559 --> 00:36:28.144
because when they do fail,

00:36:28.186 --> 00:36:31.939
and all entrepreneurs do
because they take risks,

00:36:31.981 --> 00:36:33.399
they just brush it off.

00:36:33.441 --> 00:36:36.736
They have this phenomenal
bounce back factor.

00:36:36.777 --> 00:36:41.073
Unlike other people who get
absorbed by the failure,

00:36:41.115 --> 00:36:42.491
and get stymied,

00:36:42.533 --> 00:36:44.911
and they can't do
anything else for a while,

00:36:44.952 --> 00:36:46.954
he can cope with failure.

00:36:46.996 --> 00:36:48.915
And to be able to bounce back,

00:36:48.956 --> 00:36:52.251
and then go into another
venture without blinking an eye

00:36:52.293 --> 00:36:54.086
is what it's all about.

00:36:54.128 --> 00:36:57.590
[dramatic music]

00:36:57.632 --> 00:37:00.134
[Narrator] With his companies
on the brink of collapse,

00:37:00.176 --> 00:37:03.304
doom was averted
at the last minute.

00:37:03.346 --> 00:37:06.307
Tesla was saved by investors

00:37:06.349 --> 00:37:11.354
and SpaceX was awarded a
$1.6 billion NASA contract.

00:37:14.815 --> 00:37:16.859
The SpaceX team is incredible.

00:37:16.901 --> 00:37:18.694
They are really
pushing the boundaries

00:37:18.736 --> 00:37:22.281
in terms of where we're going
forward with exploration.

00:37:22.323 --> 00:37:26.077
I think humans are
naturally driven to do this,

00:37:26.118 --> 00:37:29.080
and this is really
the beginning I think

00:37:29.121 --> 00:37:31.123
of human beings leaving
low Earth orbit.

00:37:31.165 --> 00:37:33.709
I certainly plan on
being around to see that.

00:37:34.919 --> 00:37:37.463
In 2010, Elon launched
his Dragon spacecraft

00:37:37.505 --> 00:37:39.173
to the International
Space Station.

00:37:39.215 --> 00:37:40.633
SpaceX became the first company

00:37:40.675 --> 00:37:43.135
to dock a private
spacecraft with the ISS,

00:37:43.177 --> 00:37:45.221
which was at the time amazing.

00:37:47.431 --> 00:37:48.808
[Josh] This is Mission
Control, Houston,

00:37:48.849 --> 00:37:50.351
official hatch opening time

00:37:50.393 --> 00:37:52.645
for the SpaceX
Dragon Spacecraft.

00:37:52.687 --> 00:37:56.399
Every bit of adrenaline in my
body released at that point.

00:37:56.440 --> 00:37:58.234
It's such an extremely
intense moment.

00:37:58.276 --> 00:37:59.485
Anything could have gone wrong,

00:37:59.527 --> 00:38:01.821
and everything went
right fortunately,

00:38:01.862 --> 00:38:05.658
so I felt, I feel very lucky.

00:38:05.700 --> 00:38:07.702
[Josh] Don Pettit giving
the thumbs up there,

00:38:07.743 --> 00:38:09.870
as well as the rest of the crew.

00:38:09.912 --> 00:38:12.248
Elon Musk is developing
a track record

00:38:13.374 --> 00:38:16.585
of delivering on
what he says he will.

00:38:16.627 --> 00:38:20.047
So, he already has
developed reusable rockets.

00:38:20.089 --> 00:38:22.216
He is supplying
the space station.

00:38:22.258 --> 00:38:24.802
The next stage will
be to have versions

00:38:24.844 --> 00:38:27.638
of his Dragon Spacecraft
that are human rated.

00:38:27.680 --> 00:38:30.057
So, he's actually
delivering on his promise.

00:38:31.100 --> 00:38:33.894
[dramatic music]

00:38:36.522 --> 00:38:38.524
[Narrator] After his
marriage to Justine

00:38:38.566 --> 00:38:40.735
had unraveled into divorce,

00:38:40.776 --> 00:38:43.904
Musk began dating British
actress Talulah Riley.

00:38:46.115 --> 00:38:49.952
According to Justine,
her divorce was finalized

00:38:49.994 --> 00:38:52.288
and six weeks
later she found out

00:38:52.330 --> 00:38:54.290
Elon was married
to someone else.

00:38:54.332 --> 00:38:56.584
It was an extremely
quick courting.

00:38:56.625 --> 00:38:59.962
He met her, courted her,
and then asked her to marry.

00:39:00.004 --> 00:39:02.423
They went on to have
a very wonderful,

00:39:02.465 --> 00:39:04.884
but also very
tempestuous relationship.

00:39:04.925 --> 00:39:08.220
They married, they
divorced, they remarried,

00:39:08.262 --> 00:39:10.556
they announced a divorce and
they called off their divorce,

00:39:10.598 --> 00:39:12.099
and then put the
divorce back on.

00:39:12.141 --> 00:39:14.226
It's very difficult
to keep track

00:39:14.268 --> 00:39:17.563
of Elon and Talulah's
ups and downs.

00:39:17.605 --> 00:39:20.066
They ended in a very
amicable fashion

00:39:20.107 --> 00:39:23.444
and even now Talulah says
never, never say never.

00:39:23.486 --> 00:39:25.696
They could still end
up back together again.

00:39:29.784 --> 00:39:32.578
[upbeat music]

00:39:32.620 --> 00:39:34.830
[Narrator] Over the
next several years,

00:39:34.872 --> 00:39:38.834
Tesla would take the lead
in the electric car market.

00:39:38.876 --> 00:39:42.296
Under Elon's leadership,
the company is heralded

00:39:42.338 --> 00:39:44.715
as the great American
auto industry

00:39:44.757 --> 00:39:47.259
success story of the century.

00:39:49.136 --> 00:39:51.055
This is kind of crazy
but I just learned,

00:39:51.097 --> 00:39:53.099
I was just told that,

00:39:53.140 --> 00:39:55.935
the total number of
orders for the Model 3

00:39:55.976 --> 00:39:59.438
in the past 24 hours
has now passed 150,000.

00:39:59.480 --> 00:40:00.981
[cheering]

00:40:01.023 --> 00:40:03.609
His concerns about climate
change and sustainability

00:40:03.651 --> 00:40:07.279
is what's behind all of this
push towards an electric car,

00:40:07.321 --> 00:40:09.198
not just a fancy Tesla,

00:40:09.240 --> 00:40:13.244
but also a mid-range car
that for $35,000 or less

00:40:13.285 --> 00:40:14.995
that everybody can drive.

00:40:15.037 --> 00:40:16.956
When the Model 3 was launched,

00:40:16.997 --> 00:40:18.207
there were something like

00:40:18.249 --> 00:40:20.209
400,000 people who
put money down.

00:40:20.251 --> 00:40:22.002
That's unheard of
in the car industry.

00:40:22.044 --> 00:40:23.504
You might have
some waiting list,

00:40:23.546 --> 00:40:27.675
but to wait that long for
a relatively affordable car

00:40:27.716 --> 00:40:29.093
is something we
haven't seen before.

00:40:29.135 --> 00:40:30.928
You can see the line
has been building up

00:40:30.970 --> 00:40:32.388
ever since we got here,

00:40:32.430 --> 00:40:35.141
so, it's like a never ending
line and some tourists

00:40:35.182 --> 00:40:37.893
actually came up to me
and asked what this was.

00:40:37.935 --> 00:40:40.521
And then I said it was Tesla
and they knew about it.

00:40:41.689 --> 00:40:44.233
Tesla famously
doesn't advertise.

00:40:44.275 --> 00:40:45.484
I don't think it needs to.

00:40:45.526 --> 00:40:47.445
There's such a buzz
about the brand.

00:40:48.737 --> 00:40:50.531
Things were on
track to be able to

00:40:51.699 --> 00:40:53.826
meet the Model 3
production timing

00:40:53.868 --> 00:40:55.661
in the middle of next year,

00:40:55.703 --> 00:40:59.373
and really excited
about what's happening.

00:41:01.417 --> 00:41:03.002
Tesla's value is something

00:41:03.043 --> 00:41:05.504
that has a lot of people
scratching their heads,

00:41:05.546 --> 00:41:07.506
the fact that it's a company
that's not making money,

00:41:07.548 --> 00:41:10.092
but yet it has a, a huge value.

00:41:11.260 --> 00:41:14.680
If you look at the
revenues to market cap

00:41:14.722 --> 00:41:18.851
of the car maker Honda and
the value to revenue on Tesla,

00:41:18.893 --> 00:41:21.479
Honda would be trading
at about 0.5 times.

00:41:21.520 --> 00:41:24.857
Tesla would be trading
about 7.4 times today.

00:41:24.899 --> 00:41:27.902
So, Tesla is obviously
valued a lot more

00:41:29.028 --> 00:41:31.572
given the little revenue
that it produces.

00:41:31.614 --> 00:41:32.823
Why?

00:41:32.865 --> 00:41:35.159
Because there's a lot of
goodwill in the stock,

00:41:35.201 --> 00:41:37.286
and that's because
Elon has a fan club

00:41:37.328 --> 00:41:39.163
in the investment community.

00:41:39.205 --> 00:41:41.957
And that access to
capital, that good will,

00:41:41.999 --> 00:41:45.211
that brand if you like
is wonderful for Elon,

00:41:45.252 --> 00:41:48.380
because that does allow him
to get on with other things,

00:41:48.422 --> 00:41:51.467
such as The Boring Company
or his AI ambitions

00:41:51.509 --> 00:41:55.513
or whatever else crazy
frontier driven ideas

00:41:55.554 --> 00:41:57.515
he comes up with next.

00:41:57.556 --> 00:42:00.893
The reputation Tesla has as
much to do with the product

00:42:00.935 --> 00:42:02.728
as the guy that's
running the company.

00:42:02.770 --> 00:42:04.230
Frankly, if the cars
weren't any good

00:42:04.271 --> 00:42:05.523
people wouldn't buy them,

00:42:05.564 --> 00:42:08.192
but they are good, they
are technology leaders,

00:42:08.234 --> 00:42:09.985
and that's what really
gets the following.

00:42:10.027 --> 00:42:11.487
The fact that there's
a guy like Elon

00:42:11.529 --> 00:42:14.740
running the company as well
is the icing on the cake.

00:42:14.782 --> 00:42:16.283
The general public
really appreciate

00:42:16.325 --> 00:42:17.868
that sort of candid approach

00:42:17.910 --> 00:42:19.787
to how he's running
his companies.

00:42:19.828 --> 00:42:21.956
What keeps me
intrigued about Musk

00:42:21.997 --> 00:42:23.707
is that if you look closely,

00:42:23.749 --> 00:42:27.127
you can see his signature in
the products that he makes.

00:42:27.169 --> 00:42:30.798
So, for example, the Tesla
sound system goes up to 11.

00:42:30.839 --> 00:42:32.424
It's a reference to Spinal Tap.

00:42:33.634 --> 00:42:34.802
The Ludicrous Mode,

00:42:34.843 --> 00:42:36.804
the warp speed that
you get on the screen

00:42:36.845 --> 00:42:39.265
when you unlock an Easter
egg for extra performance.

00:42:39.306 --> 00:42:40.599
It's those little details

00:42:40.641 --> 00:42:43.435
that car companies
don't normally do

00:42:43.477 --> 00:42:45.813
but Tesla just has a
bit of fun with them.

00:42:45.854 --> 00:42:47.106
It's not yet like Knight Rider,

00:42:47.147 --> 00:42:49.608
because the car doesn't
talk back at you.

00:42:49.650 --> 00:42:51.610
You can't have a
conversation with it,

00:42:51.652 --> 00:42:53.654
but when it comes to
autonomous technology,

00:42:53.696 --> 00:42:56.824
Tesla is certainly one
of the leaders out there.

00:42:56.865 --> 00:42:59.535
If people want
electric cars, fine,

00:42:59.577 --> 00:43:03.747
but you see through
electric cars comes what?

00:43:04.707 --> 00:43:06.375
Driverless cars.

00:43:06.417 --> 00:43:08.502
Now, when you see
some of the things

00:43:08.544 --> 00:43:10.004
that have happened
with driverless cars

00:43:10.045 --> 00:43:12.965
and you see how computers
can go wrong, well you know,

00:43:13.007 --> 00:43:16.051
I'm not sure I would want
to be in driverless car.

00:43:16.093 --> 00:43:17.386
People are quite keyed off

00:43:17.428 --> 00:43:19.555
over the whole
autonomous vehicle thing.

00:43:19.597 --> 00:43:21.640
I think maybe people have
this like image of like,

00:43:21.682 --> 00:43:23.225
robot cars run amok.

00:43:23.267 --> 00:43:26.061
It's really not
anything like that.

00:43:26.103 --> 00:43:27.813
All of that would apply to,

00:43:27.855 --> 00:43:29.857
the software goes through
extreme levels of validation

00:43:29.898 --> 00:43:31.317
before a customer ever sees it.

00:43:31.358 --> 00:43:32.568
So, there's all
sorts of nonsense

00:43:32.610 --> 00:43:33.819
about people being used
as human guinea pigs.

00:43:33.861 --> 00:43:35.446
You know, it's basically

00:43:35.487 --> 00:43:37.906
advanced driver's
assistance at this point,

00:43:37.948 --> 00:43:40.409
and in fact we use
the name Autopilot,

00:43:40.451 --> 00:43:43.037
because that's what that's
what's used in an aircraft,

00:43:43.078 --> 00:43:46.373
but the presumption in aircraft
is that the pilot must,

00:43:46.415 --> 00:43:47.833
you know, pay
attention and be alert

00:43:47.875 --> 00:43:49.710
and be ready to take
over at any time,

00:43:49.752 --> 00:43:52.171
not that the pilot can
then go, go to sleep,

00:43:52.212 --> 00:43:55.174
or you know walk away
from the cockpit.

00:43:55.215 --> 00:43:58.302
[car engine revving]

00:44:02.890 --> 00:44:04.224
[Narrator] Musk is confronting

00:44:04.266 --> 00:44:07.436
some of the world's
biggest challenges head on.

00:44:07.478 --> 00:44:11.732
Through Tesla, he intends to
reduce our use of fossil fuels,

00:44:11.774 --> 00:44:14.777
while simultaneously
attempting to revolutionize

00:44:14.818 --> 00:44:15.819
the energy industry.

00:44:17.029 --> 00:44:18.739
A key part of his aim

00:44:18.781 --> 00:44:21.659
is to change not just
energy consumption,

00:44:21.700 --> 00:44:23.285
but energy production.

00:44:24.411 --> 00:44:26.705
Elon is the Chairman
of SolarCity,

00:44:26.747 --> 00:44:29.708
one of the largest producers
of solar power systems

00:44:29.750 --> 00:44:31.210
in the United States.

00:44:32.586 --> 00:44:34.338
People in a remote village
or an island somewhere

00:44:34.380 --> 00:44:38.008
can take solar panels, combine
it with the Tesla Powerwall

00:44:38.050 --> 00:44:41.261
and never have to worry about
having electricity lines.

00:44:41.303 --> 00:44:42.471
Electricity lines are not

00:44:42.513 --> 00:44:43.722
the most pretty
thing in the world.

00:44:43.764 --> 00:44:45.265
So, being able to
have this solution

00:44:45.307 --> 00:44:47.226
just works wherever you are.

00:44:47.267 --> 00:44:50.312
The fact that it's
wall mounted is vital,

00:44:50.354 --> 00:44:52.981
because it means you don't
need to have a battery room.

00:44:53.023 --> 00:44:56.568
It means that a normal
household can mount this

00:44:56.610 --> 00:45:00.239
on their garage or on the
outside wall of their house

00:45:00.280 --> 00:45:02.241
and it doesn't take up any room.

00:45:02.282 --> 00:45:05.035
You could actually go if you
want completely off grid.

00:45:05.077 --> 00:45:08.205
You can take your solar panels,
charge the battery packs,

00:45:08.247 --> 00:45:12.668
and that's all you use and
the cost of this is $3500.

00:45:12.710 --> 00:45:15.421
[audience cheering]

00:45:15.462 --> 00:45:17.798
Oh wow, the grid,
it's actually zero.

00:45:17.840 --> 00:45:19.383
[Elon laughs]

00:45:19.425 --> 00:45:22.302
This entire night has
been powered by batteries.

00:45:22.344 --> 00:45:25.347
[audience cheering]

00:45:27.141 --> 00:45:29.435
With The SolarCity Company
as now part of Tesla.

00:45:29.476 --> 00:45:30.936
Elon is trying to
make solar panels

00:45:30.978 --> 00:45:32.938
more attractive to
the mass market.

00:45:32.980 --> 00:45:34.940
They launched their new
solar roof technology,

00:45:34.982 --> 00:45:36.608
whereas in the old days
solar panels would be

00:45:36.650 --> 00:45:39.611
big, black, bulky squares

00:45:39.653 --> 00:45:41.488
that you'd put out on
your roof or your garden.

00:45:41.530 --> 00:45:43.157
Now people going to
have solar panels

00:45:43.198 --> 00:45:44.491
embedded into their roof.

00:45:46.326 --> 00:45:48.162
[Narrator] Tesla's
lithium-ion battery

00:45:48.203 --> 00:45:50.122
installed in South Australia,

00:45:50.164 --> 00:45:52.624
led to a massive
drop in the cost

00:45:52.666 --> 00:45:55.794
of backing up the
region's power supply.

00:45:55.836 --> 00:45:58.046
The system powered
by wind turbines

00:45:58.088 --> 00:46:02.050
reduced the price of power
outages by 90 percent.

00:46:03.260 --> 00:46:04.678
They are taking the
surplus electricity

00:46:04.720 --> 00:46:06.305
that's being produced
in Australia,

00:46:06.346 --> 00:46:08.015
and storing it and putting
it back to the grid.

00:46:08.056 --> 00:46:09.433
I think their latest
estimate suggests

00:46:09.475 --> 00:46:11.018
they're making up to a
million dollars a day

00:46:11.059 --> 00:46:12.311
in the electricity
they're storing,

00:46:12.352 --> 00:46:13.812
which is pretty impressive.

00:46:13.854 --> 00:46:16.815
That came from a bet that
he made over Twitter.

00:46:16.857 --> 00:46:19.485
So, it was an
Australian businessman,

00:46:19.526 --> 00:46:21.445
and they were discussing
about the feasibility

00:46:21.487 --> 00:46:23.489
of making a city
run on solar power.

00:46:23.530 --> 00:46:26.074
And a bet was
issued to Musk about

00:46:26.116 --> 00:46:27.826
can you do that
in this much time,

00:46:27.868 --> 00:46:29.495
and he went, I'll take that bet.

00:46:30.370 --> 00:46:32.623
[keyboard keys chattering]

00:46:32.664 --> 00:46:33.874
He put the batteries in place,

00:46:33.916 --> 00:46:35.542
had it up and running
within the time scale,

00:46:35.584 --> 00:46:38.504
all for a billionaire's bet
and that's what came of it.

00:46:39.713 --> 00:46:42.299
We have reached an agreement

00:46:42.341 --> 00:46:44.468
with Tesla Motor Company

00:46:44.510 --> 00:46:48.764
subject to legislative
review and approval,

00:46:48.806 --> 00:46:53.519
that will enable Tesla to
build the world's largest

00:46:53.560 --> 00:46:55.938
and most advanced
battery factory,

00:46:55.979 --> 00:46:58.607
right here in the Silver State.

00:46:58.649 --> 00:47:00.651
[audience cheering]

00:47:00.692 --> 00:47:02.277
Why do we even call
it the Gigafactory?

00:47:02.319 --> 00:47:04.571
It's because, it's
actually gonna be,

00:47:04.613 --> 00:47:06.031
it's not just gonna be

00:47:06.073 --> 00:47:07.908
the biggest lithium-ion
battery factory in the world,

00:47:07.950 --> 00:47:09.576
but it will actually
be bigger than the sum

00:47:09.618 --> 00:47:12.329
of all lithium-ion
factories in the world.

00:47:12.371 --> 00:47:14.498
So, it's difficult to
quite describe in words,

00:47:14.540 --> 00:47:16.375
but it's a heck
of a big factory.

00:47:16.416 --> 00:47:19.419
[audience cheering]

00:47:20.379 --> 00:47:23.131
[dramatic music]

00:47:24.758 --> 00:47:26.301
[Narrator] Since
the Dragon launch,

00:47:26.343 --> 00:47:30.556
Elon Musk has continued to
make history with SpaceX.

00:47:30.597 --> 00:47:32.432
When the Falcon Heavy rocket

00:47:32.474 --> 00:47:34.726
launched in February 2018,

00:47:34.768 --> 00:47:37.896
it heralded the start
of a new space race.

00:47:37.938 --> 00:47:39.606
[rocket engines rumbling]

00:47:39.648 --> 00:47:41.108
[Phil] This is a big deal.

00:47:41.149 --> 00:47:43.777
This is kind of the Super
Bowl of rocket launches.

00:47:45.237 --> 00:47:47.531
Elon Musk and his
team just proved

00:47:47.573 --> 00:47:48.949
that the Falcon Heavy works,

00:47:48.991 --> 00:47:51.743
which puts us now in a
completely different realm,

00:47:51.785 --> 00:47:53.328
what we can put into deep space,

00:47:53.370 --> 00:47:54.663
what we can send to Mars.

00:47:55.831 --> 00:47:57.791
[Narrator] Lifting off
from the same launch pad

00:47:57.833 --> 00:48:01.044
that sent the crew of
Apollo 11 to the moon,

00:48:01.086 --> 00:48:03.422
the mission
represented a new era

00:48:03.463 --> 00:48:06.091
in the progress of space flight.

00:48:06.133 --> 00:48:08.844
[majestic music]

00:48:11.388 --> 00:48:13.974
The launch of the
SpaceX Falcon Heavy,

00:48:14.016 --> 00:48:17.311
it was significant for so
many reasons scientifically.

00:48:17.352 --> 00:48:19.605
I mean it's the most powerful
rocket we've seen launched

00:48:19.646 --> 00:48:22.149
since the Saturn V, which
took humans to the moon.

00:48:24.026 --> 00:48:26.945
[Neil] It's one
small step for man,

00:48:26.987 --> 00:48:31.158
one giant leap for mankind.

00:48:31.199 --> 00:48:35.329
We are entering a new
era of space exploration,

00:48:35.370 --> 00:48:37.039
which is extremely exciting.

00:48:37.080 --> 00:48:39.458
Sort of the continuance
of the dream of Apollo.

00:48:40.584 --> 00:48:42.628
I was on the Space
Coast for the launch.

00:48:42.669 --> 00:48:44.796
I haven't seen it that
busy there since 2011

00:48:44.838 --> 00:48:45.964
when the space shuttle launched

00:48:46.006 --> 00:48:48.133
and landed for the final time.

00:48:48.175 --> 00:48:51.011
It really has started to
capture people's imaginations

00:48:51.053 --> 00:48:53.305
about space exploration.

00:48:53.347 --> 00:48:56.725
It was a great achievement
by a private company

00:48:56.767 --> 00:48:59.686
to create and
successfully launch

00:48:59.728 --> 00:49:04.733
one of the biggest lifting
rockets ever in history.

00:49:06.109 --> 00:49:07.819
It's the third largest
rocket ever to be launched.

00:49:07.861 --> 00:49:10.155
We had the Apollo Saturn V,

00:49:10.197 --> 00:49:11.657
and of course we
had the shuttle.

00:49:11.698 --> 00:49:14.826
The Falcon Heavy is
that order of magnitude.

00:49:14.868 --> 00:49:18.455
It is a seriously big rocket.

00:49:18.497 --> 00:49:21.333
The Falcon Heavy
can carry 64 tons.

00:49:21.375 --> 00:49:23.835
That's a lot, I
mean it's a truck.

00:49:23.877 --> 00:49:27.547
It's the equivalent
of a 737 jetliner,

00:49:27.589 --> 00:49:28.924
filled with passengers,

00:49:28.966 --> 00:49:30.676
filled with baggage,
filled with fuel,

00:49:30.717 --> 00:49:33.011
lifting that up and
sending it into space,

00:49:33.053 --> 00:49:34.638
and not just sending
it into space,

00:49:34.680 --> 00:49:36.306
sending it beyond
low Earth orbit,

00:49:36.348 --> 00:49:39.142
sending it beyond the moon,
sending it beyond Mars.

00:49:39.184 --> 00:49:42.896
This launch will also have
political implications

00:49:42.938 --> 00:49:44.106
in my view,

00:49:44.147 --> 00:49:47.526
because the American
government is preparing

00:49:47.567 --> 00:49:49.695
and budgeting for
NASA's rockets,

00:49:49.736 --> 00:49:53.198
but the fact that Elon
and SpaceX have now proven

00:49:53.240 --> 00:49:56.368
they can successfully
launch the Falcon Heavy,

00:49:56.410 --> 00:49:59.579
which will probably have a
cost at about a $100 million,

00:49:59.621 --> 00:50:02.499
that's about 10% of
the NASA rockets,

00:50:02.541 --> 00:50:05.335
so they are budgeting about
a billion dollars per launch.

00:50:05.377 --> 00:50:07.212
That's a significant
cost saving,

00:50:07.254 --> 00:50:09.881
and that is what a
private enterprise can do

00:50:09.923 --> 00:50:12.092
when you put the right
person at the helm

00:50:12.134 --> 00:50:14.761
and you give him a chance
to execute his vision.

00:50:16.555 --> 00:50:17.556
In order for us to

00:50:18.765 --> 00:50:21.226
really open up access to space

00:50:21.268 --> 00:50:25.397
we've got to achieve full
and rapid reusability,

00:50:25.439 --> 00:50:28.567
and being able to do that for
the primary rocket booster

00:50:28.608 --> 00:50:31.737
is gonna be a huge
impact on the cost.

00:50:31.778 --> 00:50:34.823
Now this will still take us a
few years to make that smooth,

00:50:34.865 --> 00:50:37.534
and make it efficient,

00:50:37.576 --> 00:50:40.912
but it's, I think it's
proven that it can work.

00:50:40.954 --> 00:50:43.957
[suspenseful music]

00:50:45.292 --> 00:50:46.918
[rocket engine roaring]

00:50:46.960 --> 00:50:48.837
[Narrator] Of all
Musk's innovations

00:50:48.879 --> 00:50:52.841
developing reusable rockets
could be the most radical.

00:50:54.009 --> 00:50:55.677
We thought it was
more likely than not

00:50:55.719 --> 00:50:57.345
that this mission would work,

00:50:57.387 --> 00:51:01.183
but still probably, you know
one third chance of failure.

00:51:01.224 --> 00:51:02.642
The Holy Grail for space

00:51:02.684 --> 00:51:06.104
is to have a totally
reusable launch system.

00:51:06.146 --> 00:51:09.524
So, to actually see the
SpaceX Falcon rockets

00:51:09.566 --> 00:51:11.443
launching its
spacecraft into orbit

00:51:11.485 --> 00:51:13.111
and then coming back down

00:51:13.153 --> 00:51:16.156
and performing a
pinpoint accurate landing

00:51:16.198 --> 00:51:20.452
on a floating platform at sea,
that is pretty astonishing.

00:51:20.494 --> 00:51:21.953
It's quite a tiny target.

00:51:21.995 --> 00:51:24.081
It's really trying to land
on a postage stamp there.

00:51:24.122 --> 00:51:25.707
Just to put it this way,

00:51:25.749 --> 00:51:28.293
you get on an airplane
and you fly somewhere,

00:51:28.335 --> 00:51:30.629
you wouldn't dream of
throwing that airplane away,

00:51:30.670 --> 00:51:33.090
so why do we do the
same with space rockets?

00:51:33.131 --> 00:51:36.218
That's what SpaceX is trying
to do and they're succeeding.

00:51:36.259 --> 00:51:38.178
They're leading the
way at the moment.

00:51:38.220 --> 00:51:39.638
Elon is getting
people to question

00:51:39.679 --> 00:51:41.807
why NASA is still building
any rockets at all.

00:51:41.848 --> 00:51:44.017
They think if SpaceX can do it,

00:51:44.059 --> 00:51:46.144
why do we need to buy
a comparable rocket?

00:51:46.186 --> 00:51:47.854
We can just buy
that one off them.

00:51:49.022 --> 00:51:51.316
Inside the Falcon Heavy
rocket was a plaque

00:51:51.358 --> 00:51:54.361
with 6,000 names of all
the SpaceX employees

00:51:54.402 --> 00:51:56.196
that worked on the project,

00:51:56.238 --> 00:51:59.783
and that just shows
that he's willing to

00:51:59.825 --> 00:52:02.369
really value the people
that work for him.

00:52:03.453 --> 00:52:06.540
Elon Musk's aspiration
is to eventually

00:52:06.581 --> 00:52:08.041
put all of his efforts

00:52:08.083 --> 00:52:11.253
into developing
far larger rockets,

00:52:11.294 --> 00:52:14.339
which can still launch
payloads into Earth orbit,

00:52:14.381 --> 00:52:18.218
but also be capable of
taking human rated spacecraft

00:52:18.260 --> 00:52:20.887
into deep space, i.e. Mars.

00:52:27.227 --> 00:52:28.979
You have like a 10 to
20 year horizon here

00:52:29.020 --> 00:52:30.397
when you want to go to Mars.

00:52:31.898 --> 00:52:33.108
-I wanna enable--
-SpaceX.

00:52:33.150 --> 00:52:35.443
I wanna enable large
numbers of people

00:52:35.485 --> 00:52:37.654
and cargo to go to Mars.

00:52:37.696 --> 00:52:39.531
So, it's not sort of
about me personally

00:52:39.573 --> 00:52:41.199
want to make a
journey back to Mars,

00:52:41.241 --> 00:52:42.701
I mean that would be
nice on a personal level,

00:52:42.742 --> 00:52:44.911
but I do think it is important

00:52:46.246 --> 00:52:49.541
that we as a species
of this civilization

00:52:49.583 --> 00:52:53.712
are on a path to become a
true space ranked civilization

00:52:54.588 --> 00:52:56.089
and a multi planet species.

00:52:59.342 --> 00:53:00.385
[Narrator] Musk aims to launch

00:53:00.427 --> 00:53:03.430
a mission to Mars by 2022.

00:53:04.598 --> 00:53:07.225
Elon Musk's ultimate
goal is to have humans

00:53:07.267 --> 00:53:09.519
permanently living
and working on Mars,

00:53:09.561 --> 00:53:12.480
a Martian colony you could say.

00:53:12.522 --> 00:53:14.733
Mars is very special.

00:53:14.774 --> 00:53:18.570
It has a sky and
it has landscape

00:53:18.612 --> 00:53:23.617
and large amounts of frozen
water and it has seasons,

00:53:24.784 --> 00:53:26.953
so there are a lot of
similarities to Earth.

00:53:28.622 --> 00:53:29.581
The atmosphere is much thinner

00:53:29.623 --> 00:53:31.082
than the Earth's atmosphere,

00:53:31.124 --> 00:53:34.169
but you've got carbon and
then you've got oxygen,

00:53:34.211 --> 00:53:35.378
so you could potentially use

00:53:35.420 --> 00:53:38.757
what's on Mars's
atmosphere as resources.

00:53:38.798 --> 00:53:41.468
It's a very terrestrial
environment.

00:53:41.509 --> 00:53:44.137
It appears to be a home
from home but is it?

00:53:47.515 --> 00:53:49.267
It is possible in our lifetime

00:53:49.309 --> 00:53:52.437
to send a rocket
to Mars, manned,

00:53:52.479 --> 00:53:54.147
I do think that's possible.

00:53:55.023 --> 00:53:56.733
Do we wanna do it?

00:53:56.775 --> 00:53:58.026
Do we wanna pay for it?

00:53:58.068 --> 00:53:59.527
That's a different question.

00:53:59.569 --> 00:54:01.696
There's a lot of questions,
there's a lot of hurdles,

00:54:01.738 --> 00:54:03.740
but as long as
Elon doesn't break

00:54:03.782 --> 00:54:06.076
the fundamental laws of
physics we can do it.

00:54:07.494 --> 00:54:11.623
Mars is reachable and I'm
sure we will devise the means

00:54:11.665 --> 00:54:13.625
by which we can live there,

00:54:13.667 --> 00:54:16.753
but there are many
questions and challenges

00:54:16.795 --> 00:54:18.421
before that can be achieved.

00:54:18.463 --> 00:54:20.966
So, let Musk say
he's gonna do it,

00:54:21.007 --> 00:54:22.842
because he probably will.

00:54:22.884 --> 00:54:27.806
If he maintains this
momentum, I see no reason

00:54:27.847 --> 00:54:31.393
why he shouldn't be landing
spacecrafts on Mars.

00:54:33.103 --> 00:54:35.855
[dramatic music]

00:54:37.023 --> 00:54:39.192
[Narrator] Elon Musk's
evolvement with design,

00:54:39.234 --> 00:54:42.404
engineering and critical
technical decisions

00:54:42.445 --> 00:54:44.823
is unique amongst his peers.

00:54:46.032 --> 00:54:48.785
Believe me, Elon is just
as smart on engineering

00:54:48.827 --> 00:54:50.704
as he is on the money side.

00:54:50.745 --> 00:54:53.790
Even in the earlier
days of Tesla and SpaceX

00:54:53.832 --> 00:54:55.667
he could easily have said,

00:54:55.709 --> 00:54:57.836
let me put in some
of my cash in there,

00:54:57.877 --> 00:55:00.005
and we'll put some
clever technical people,

00:55:00.046 --> 00:55:02.299
and I'll just run it,
I'll be the manager,

00:55:02.340 --> 00:55:04.759
I'll be the CEO or
whatever, uhn-uhn.

00:55:04.801 --> 00:55:06.386
To actually be able
to get so involved

00:55:06.428 --> 00:55:08.722
in the engineering
side, that's amazing,

00:55:08.763 --> 00:55:10.015
but that's what he did,

00:55:10.056 --> 00:55:12.475
because deep down
he is an engineer.

00:55:12.517 --> 00:55:15.645
I would say he approaches
engineering and business

00:55:15.687 --> 00:55:19.691
in much the same fashion,
it's a puzzle to be dissected,

00:55:19.733 --> 00:55:23.194
and then the pieces put
back together in some way.

00:55:23.236 --> 00:55:25.655
At his heart, the
man is an engineer.

00:55:25.697 --> 00:55:28.074
I think that's where
his first love is.

00:55:28.116 --> 00:55:29.034
He'll be walking around SpaceX,

00:55:29.075 --> 00:55:31.244
and will talk to the employees,

00:55:31.286 --> 00:55:34.289
and then will quiz them on
things like thrust vectors

00:55:34.331 --> 00:55:36.541
or aero dynamical design,

00:55:36.583 --> 00:55:39.711
not typical of CEO behavior.

00:55:39.753 --> 00:55:41.921
I think the engineering
came more naturally

00:55:41.963 --> 00:55:43.381
because the engineering part

00:55:43.423 --> 00:55:45.592
is a more rational approach.

00:55:45.633 --> 00:55:49.512
He sort of struggled early on

00:55:49.554 --> 00:55:53.141
in the CEO role of his
two early companies,

00:55:53.183 --> 00:55:56.019
but certainly we've seen
that he just learned

00:55:56.061 --> 00:55:59.439
and grew from that, and
so I think you know,

00:55:59.481 --> 00:56:02.901
he's sort of the whole
package at this point.

00:56:02.942 --> 00:56:05.528
I don't think too many
people would argue with that.

00:56:06.946 --> 00:56:10.658
Some entrepreneurs are more
socially skilled than others,

00:56:10.700 --> 00:56:13.078
but they're not necessarily
socially skilled,

00:56:13.119 --> 00:56:14.412
because they're too focused.

00:56:14.454 --> 00:56:17.332
You see, if you're a
socially skilled human being,

00:56:17.374 --> 00:56:19.751
then you're sensitive
to everybody around you,

00:56:19.793 --> 00:56:21.878
but that may prevent you
from actually getting on

00:56:21.920 --> 00:56:24.339
to achieve your objective,

00:56:24.381 --> 00:56:26.257
and they have
objectives in mind,

00:56:26.299 --> 00:56:28.385
and they are
ruthless in the sense

00:56:28.426 --> 00:56:30.887
of trying to achieve
that objective.

00:56:30.929 --> 00:56:34.391
Not ruthless in the way they
treat other people necessarily,

00:56:34.432 --> 00:56:38.103
just in making sure that
they're on their own timeline

00:56:38.144 --> 00:56:40.230
to achieve that objective.

00:56:42.774 --> 00:56:45.610
[upbeat music]

00:56:45.652 --> 00:56:48.363
I had made an
enormous PR mistake,

00:56:48.405 --> 00:56:51.324
and it had landed on the front
page of the New York post,

00:56:51.366 --> 00:56:55.412
and it was bad, like it
was mortifyingly bad,

00:56:55.453 --> 00:56:57.288
and when I went into
tell Elon about it,

00:56:57.330 --> 00:56:59.499
you know, he was very,
very angry with me,

00:56:59.541 --> 00:57:00.875
and the things that he said,

00:57:00.917 --> 00:57:03.420
the thing that he said
that stuck with me was,

00:57:03.461 --> 00:57:06.172
Julie, perception
becomes reality.

00:57:06.214 --> 00:57:08.216
It was the only time
that he was really,

00:57:08.258 --> 00:57:10.135
really angry with me
that I can remember.

00:57:10.176 --> 00:57:12.137
And the fact that
he didn't fire me,

00:57:12.178 --> 00:57:14.681
I think just points to
him being a nice guy.

00:57:17.517 --> 00:57:20.186
One of the things I find
so fascinating about Elon

00:57:20.228 --> 00:57:24.691
is that in the midst of all
his acumen and business sense

00:57:24.732 --> 00:57:28.486
he does interviews where
he tears up and even cries

00:57:28.528 --> 00:57:32.115
talking about how he
wants to find the one.

00:57:32.157 --> 00:57:34.617
It's not easy being Elon.

00:57:34.659 --> 00:57:36.744
It sounds amazing,
it sounds glamorous,

00:57:36.786 --> 00:57:39.664
and it no doubt is
also a lot of fun.

00:57:39.706 --> 00:57:43.126
He's having a ball executing
his engineering dreams.

00:57:43.168 --> 00:57:45.628
The flip side of that comes
with the personal side,

00:57:45.670 --> 00:57:46.838
it ain't easy.

00:57:48.756 --> 00:57:50.216
Elon started dating

00:57:50.258 --> 00:57:53.511
a very famous celebrity
actress who was at the time

00:57:53.553 --> 00:57:56.598
at war with her
ex-husband Johnny Depp.

00:57:56.639 --> 00:57:58.683
Like, I don't know
what it is about Amber,

00:57:58.725 --> 00:58:01.519
but men who fall
in love with Amber,

00:58:01.561 --> 00:58:04.189
they fall hard for Amber.

00:58:04.230 --> 00:58:07.025
So, everyone was very
curious, in the showbiz world,

00:58:07.066 --> 00:58:10.069
to see who Amber would
end up with next,

00:58:10.111 --> 00:58:12.322
and when we saw she
was with a billionaire,

00:58:12.363 --> 00:58:14.699
who had this kind of baby face,

00:58:14.741 --> 00:58:17.660
there was a lot of interest
in finding out more.

00:58:18.745 --> 00:58:21.039
The breakup between
Elon and Amber

00:58:21.080 --> 00:58:22.749
was very painful for Elon,

00:58:22.790 --> 00:58:25.043
and he very
characteristically I suppose

00:58:25.084 --> 00:58:27.337
is very open with
people and journalists

00:58:27.378 --> 00:58:28.838
about how much he was hurting.

00:58:28.880 --> 00:58:31.674
At one stage, in a very
high profile interview

00:58:31.716 --> 00:58:33.051
he did with Rolling Stone,

00:58:33.092 --> 00:58:36.596
he actually asked the journalist
who was doing the story,

00:58:36.638 --> 00:58:38.848
do you know anybody you
could set me up with?

00:58:38.890 --> 00:58:41.142
Is there anybody you
know I should date?

00:58:41.184 --> 00:58:44.103
For a billionaire guy, who
is like, let's be honest,

00:58:44.145 --> 00:58:48.566
decently good looking,
to be out there talking

00:58:48.608 --> 00:58:51.402
about how he wants to
find a nice girlfriend,

00:58:51.444 --> 00:58:54.697
it's just something you don't
see come along very often.

00:58:54.739 --> 00:58:56.574
He's fairly an open book.

00:58:56.616 --> 00:58:58.910
So, if they had been
spending some time together

00:58:58.952 --> 00:59:02.747
and the journalist had earned
his trust in some capacity,

00:59:02.789 --> 00:59:04.207
it wouldn't surprise me at all.

00:59:04.249 --> 00:59:06.918
I mean, he definitely wears
his heart on his sleeve.

00:59:06.960 --> 00:59:09.504
I would say he's a
big softie underneath.

00:59:10.630 --> 00:59:12.924
I mean Elon is so
honest and vulnerable,

00:59:12.966 --> 00:59:14.509
and I think that's
why people find him

00:59:14.551 --> 00:59:16.427
so incredibly endearing.

00:59:16.469 --> 00:59:19.764
There are a lot of
technological dreams

00:59:19.806 --> 00:59:21.975
that Elon Musk would
have for himself

00:59:22.016 --> 00:59:23.560
over the next two decades,

00:59:23.601 --> 00:59:25.728
but I think as much as
he wants all of that,

00:59:25.770 --> 00:59:29.607
I think Elon Musk would
like to be happily in love.

00:59:29.649 --> 00:59:33.069
[electronic dance music]

00:59:35.488 --> 00:59:38.157
[Narrator] Musk continues
to invest in the future.

00:59:39.492 --> 00:59:43.329
In 2013, he outlined a
potential world changing plan

00:59:43.371 --> 00:59:45.123
for a transportation system

00:59:45.164 --> 00:59:48.251
that could reshape the
way humanity travels.

00:59:50.503 --> 00:59:52.005
Elon Musk came up with an idea.

00:59:52.046 --> 00:59:54.924
He published a white paper
on this vacuum transport,

00:59:54.966 --> 00:59:56.092
but let it go.

00:59:56.134 --> 00:59:57.594
He said I'm not
gonna do this myself.

00:59:57.635 --> 01:00:00.138
What I'm gonna do is open
up to third party companies

01:00:00.179 --> 01:00:02.515
that want to develop
it themselves.

01:00:02.557 --> 01:00:05.101
The Hyperloop project
is of huge interest

01:00:05.143 --> 01:00:06.936
in terms of the world
of transportation,

01:00:06.978 --> 01:00:10.565
the idea of going from San
Francisco to Los Angeles

01:00:10.607 --> 01:00:13.192
in the time it
would take to drive

01:00:13.234 --> 01:00:15.778
from San Francisco to
the edge of San Francisco

01:00:15.820 --> 01:00:17.030
is fantastic.

01:00:17.071 --> 01:00:18.823
It's a vacuum tube,
where all the air

01:00:18.865 --> 01:00:20.700
is sucked out of the
tube, and you send a pod

01:00:20.742 --> 01:00:22.619
containing passengers
through the tube.

01:00:22.660 --> 01:00:24.495
There's no friction,
there's no air resistance,

01:00:24.537 --> 01:00:26.664
because the pods are
levitating on magnets.

01:00:26.706 --> 01:00:28.207
-The technology is sound.

01:00:28.249 --> 01:00:30.793
We can travel faster in a
vacuum, there's no friction.

01:00:30.835 --> 01:00:31.836
They want to get up to speeds

01:00:31.878 --> 01:00:34.505
about 500 to 700 miles an hour.

01:00:34.547 --> 01:00:37.133
Elon's idea is that tubes
could connect major cities,

01:00:37.175 --> 01:00:39.052
and you could travel in
one of these passenger pods

01:00:39.093 --> 01:00:41.346
and travel from, say, 
L.A. to New York

01:00:41.387 --> 01:00:43.014
in a matter of half an hour.

01:00:43.056 --> 01:00:44.390
When he was talking
about it the first time,

01:00:44.432 --> 01:00:46.976
we all thought oh well
this time he might have,

01:00:47.018 --> 01:00:48.686
he might have lost
it a little bit,

01:00:48.728 --> 01:00:52.315
or it's a little bit too
ambitious or too crazy,

01:00:52.357 --> 01:00:56.194
but, as with many other of
Elon's ideas, watch that space,

01:00:56.235 --> 01:00:57.695
because before you know it,

01:00:57.737 --> 01:00:59.530
and actually ahead of schedule,

01:00:59.572 --> 01:01:01.824
this thing is actually
becoming reality.

01:01:04.744 --> 01:01:08.414
The speed we can put these
tunnels out is dramatic,

01:01:08.456 --> 01:01:10.041
how fast it can all happen.

01:01:10.083 --> 01:01:13.002
So, I think a lot of the
world will be connected.

01:01:14.253 --> 01:01:17.382
We'll be moving cargo in 2019.

01:01:17.423 --> 01:01:19.217
We think we can have passengers

01:01:19.258 --> 01:01:23.221
safely being transported
in Hyperloop by 2021.

01:01:26.599 --> 01:01:28.393
[Narrator] Elon Musk
is a leading voice

01:01:28.434 --> 01:01:31.604
on the potential of
an AI apocalypse.

01:01:31.646 --> 01:01:34.857
He has declared that
digital super intelligence

01:01:34.899 --> 01:01:36.609
is the most serious threat

01:01:36.651 --> 01:01:38.903
to the survival
of the human race.

01:01:40.863 --> 01:01:43.866
To offset this, he
launched Neuralink,

01:01:43.908 --> 01:01:45.243
aimed to developing safe

01:01:45.284 --> 01:01:47.954
and beneficial
artificial intelligence.

01:01:49.789 --> 01:01:51.958
[Per] The dangers of AI,

01:01:51.999 --> 01:01:55.378
are the end of the human
mind as we know it.

01:01:55.420 --> 01:01:56.838
You're dealing with something

01:01:56.879 --> 01:01:58.589
that is fundamentally going
to be smarter than you.

01:01:58.631 --> 01:01:59.966
It's gonna learn on its own,

01:02:00.007 --> 01:02:01.801
so how do you stay ahead of it?

01:02:01.843 --> 01:02:04.387
That's something that Musk
is paying close attention to.

01:02:04.429 --> 01:02:05.596
His main concern is that,

01:02:05.638 --> 01:02:07.890
if AI becomes too smart
and too important,

01:02:07.932 --> 01:02:09.726
then why do you need humans?

01:02:09.767 --> 01:02:11.144
We kind of become obsolete.

01:02:12.437 --> 01:02:14.981
The very worst nightmare

01:02:15.022 --> 01:02:18.192
that a Hollywood horror
movie could come up with

01:02:18.234 --> 01:02:21.571
is as nothing compared
to where we're heading

01:02:21.612 --> 01:02:23.364
and heading so fast.

01:02:23.406 --> 01:02:26.784
[dramatic music]

01:02:26.826 --> 01:02:31.581
When AI has the
ability to think,

01:02:31.622 --> 01:02:36.210
to come to conclusions, and
to make judgments, et cetera,

01:02:36.252 --> 01:02:38.212
just like the human mind,

01:02:38.254 --> 01:02:42.341
does anyone think they might
just be the slightest chance

01:02:42.383 --> 01:02:46.679
that through that alone, AI
could take the planet over?

01:02:49.724 --> 01:02:51.768
If they do become self-aware,

01:02:51.809 --> 01:02:52.977
how are we gonna control them?

01:02:53.019 --> 01:02:54.979
Who's gonna control
the controllers?

01:02:55.021 --> 01:02:56.355
It's a scary prospect.

01:02:57.774 --> 01:03:01.402
What is its potential
to take over everything?

01:03:01.444 --> 01:03:03.404
You know what its potential
to take over everything is?

01:03:03.446 --> 01:03:07.617
100 percent, it's
the end of humanity.

01:03:07.658 --> 01:03:10.661
When software rises up
and becomes all powerful,

01:03:10.703 --> 01:03:11.913
where are we gonna look to?

01:03:11.954 --> 01:03:13.623
We're gonna look to
the titans of industry

01:03:13.664 --> 01:03:15.166
to provide a counterweight.

01:03:15.208 --> 01:03:18.961
Elon is ideally placed to
be the sort of counterpoint

01:03:19.003 --> 01:03:21.172
to this software early vision.

01:03:21.214 --> 01:03:22.340
I mean it's interesting

01:03:22.381 --> 01:03:24.759
that he's also looking
into AI himself

01:03:24.801 --> 01:03:26.719
with open AI and Neuralink.

01:03:27.929 --> 01:03:32.016
When I hear Elon Musk
talking about the fact

01:03:32.058 --> 01:03:36.854
that AI revolution is
humanity summoning the demon,

01:03:36.896 --> 01:03:39.190
absolutely it is,

01:03:39.232 --> 01:03:42.902
but then founding and opening
company called Neuralink

01:03:42.944 --> 01:03:46.113
to connect AI to
the human brain.

01:03:46.155 --> 01:03:49.534
I mean that contradiction
is so extraordinary,

01:03:49.575 --> 01:03:51.744
I don't know the
thought processes

01:03:51.786 --> 01:03:53.204
that must be going on there.

01:03:54.288 --> 01:03:56.457
Neuralink is a direct response

01:03:56.499 --> 01:03:59.043
of Elon's concerns to
AI in the first place.

01:03:59.085 --> 01:04:01.337
The fact that he thinks
AI might surpass humans

01:04:01.379 --> 01:04:03.548
in terms of its
mental capabilities,

01:04:03.589 --> 01:04:06.092
it makes sense that he would
try and develop technology

01:04:06.133 --> 01:04:08.469
using computers in
tandem with our biology.

01:04:09.846 --> 01:04:12.932
I think that Elon
Musk wants to use AI

01:04:12.974 --> 01:04:17.645
as a tool for
augmenting our self,

01:04:17.687 --> 01:04:20.606
and not a tool for
replacing our self.

01:04:20.648 --> 01:04:23.234
I think he's saying if
you can't beat them,

01:04:23.276 --> 01:04:26.612
become superhuman
and then beat them.

01:04:26.654 --> 01:04:29.615
Brain computer interfaces
could help people

01:04:29.657 --> 01:04:30.825
in two different ways.

01:04:30.867 --> 01:04:34.120
So, one way would be
to restore capabilities

01:04:34.161 --> 01:04:36.247
for people with disabilities,

01:04:36.289 --> 01:04:38.332
and another application would

01:04:38.374 --> 01:04:40.960
be to go beyond our limitation.

01:04:41.002 --> 01:04:43.337
And so, for example
we could think

01:04:43.379 --> 01:04:47.508
about controlling a computer
just with our thoughts

01:04:47.550 --> 01:04:49.510
without moving any muscle,

01:04:49.552 --> 01:04:52.430
and that's the core
function of a BCI,

01:04:52.471 --> 01:04:54.515
a brain-computer interface.

01:04:54.557 --> 01:04:56.392
There are a lot of
different scenarios

01:04:56.434 --> 01:04:58.269
in which you could
apply this technology.

01:04:58.311 --> 01:05:00.229
We are talking about critical

01:05:00.271 --> 01:05:02.899
decision making in the military,

01:05:02.940 --> 01:05:06.819
in a warfare scenario,
or in the health sector.

01:05:06.861 --> 01:05:10.281
So, whenever we talk
about hybrid humans,

01:05:10.323 --> 01:05:13.451
or human and
machine, or cyborgs,

01:05:13.492 --> 01:05:16.537
we are all talking about
the same thing I think,

01:05:16.579 --> 01:05:21.375
which is using technology
to augment our capabilities

01:05:22.335 --> 01:05:23.169
and that's it.

01:05:25.922 --> 01:05:27.131
[Narrator] Musk's skepticism

01:05:27.173 --> 01:05:30.176
about the potential
risks of AI technology

01:05:30.217 --> 01:05:33.179
has led to a public clash
with Mark Zuckerberg.

01:05:34.972 --> 01:05:37.475
I am optimistic about
the future of AI.

01:05:37.516 --> 01:05:40.645
I think that AI
is going to unlock

01:05:40.686 --> 01:05:43.606
a huge amount of
positive things,

01:05:43.648 --> 01:05:47.193
whether that's helping to
identify and cure diseases,

01:05:47.234 --> 01:05:49.987
to help cars drive more safely,

01:05:50.029 --> 01:05:51.989
to help keep our
communities safe,

01:05:52.031 --> 01:05:54.033
are there going to be
issues along the way?

01:05:54.075 --> 01:05:55.284
Of course there are.

01:05:55.326 --> 01:05:57.578
No technology comes
out fully formed,

01:05:57.620 --> 01:06:00.373
and we need the pioneers to
be able to go work on this

01:06:00.414 --> 01:06:02.708
and get to the
state that we want.

01:06:06.045 --> 01:06:07.546
I do think it's very healthy

01:06:07.588 --> 01:06:11.258
that we have such
technology giants

01:06:11.300 --> 01:06:13.094
fighting it out publicly,

01:06:13.135 --> 01:06:15.262
so that we can all form
an opinion about it.

01:06:15.304 --> 01:06:18.182
It's a spectator sport
now for the rest of us.

01:06:18.224 --> 01:06:20.267
Mark Zuckerberg sees
Elon as a naysayer

01:06:20.309 --> 01:06:23.771
and someone who's trying to
stop the development of AI.

01:06:23.813 --> 01:06:25.856
I can see both sides
of the argument.

01:06:25.898 --> 01:06:28.442
I'd probably slide slightly
with Mark Zuckerberg

01:06:28.484 --> 01:06:30.736
in the sense that I think we
will be able to control AI

01:06:30.778 --> 01:06:33.322
enough to keep humanity safe.

01:06:33.364 --> 01:06:35.324
I wouldn't bet against Elon.

01:06:35.366 --> 01:06:37.910
You know if Elon thinks
that AI is gonna take over

01:06:37.952 --> 01:06:39.078
and eventually decide

01:06:39.120 --> 01:06:41.998
that us irrational
humans are unnecessary,

01:06:42.039 --> 01:06:43.207
I would trust him.

01:06:43.249 --> 01:06:45.418
You know, he hasn't
been wrong about a lot.

01:06:45.459 --> 01:06:48.379
The debate is either it's a
good thing, we must do it.

01:06:48.421 --> 01:06:52.299
It's a bad thing, but we still
must do it in another way.

01:06:52.341 --> 01:06:55.094
We should stop
doing it immediately

01:06:55.136 --> 01:06:58.889
before the line is crossed
where humanity is no more.

01:06:58.931 --> 01:06:59.932
We need policies.

01:06:59.974 --> 01:07:01.684
We need to start thinking,

01:07:01.726 --> 01:07:05.563
what is a good application
of BCI and what is not.

01:07:06.731 --> 01:07:09.233
We need to be the
one with the control,

01:07:09.275 --> 01:07:10.818
being able to push a button,

01:07:10.860 --> 01:07:12.820
and disable the
AI at any moment.

01:07:14.572 --> 01:07:16.407
Artificial intelligence
can generate

01:07:16.449 --> 01:07:18.409
quite successful content,

01:07:18.451 --> 01:07:21.078
however, rather than
replacing humans,

01:07:21.120 --> 01:07:23.456
I'd prefer to see
us working together.

01:07:23.497 --> 01:07:27.126
We both bring unique skills
and talents to the table.

01:07:27.168 --> 01:07:28.419
There's gonna be a difference

01:07:28.461 --> 01:07:30.212
between what's doable
and what's acceptable,

01:07:30.254 --> 01:07:33.424
and this is something that
Elon is not gonna decide,

01:07:33.466 --> 01:07:34.925
this is not something
that Mark Zuckerberg

01:07:34.967 --> 01:07:36.761
is not gonna decide.

01:07:36.802 --> 01:07:39.180
This is you and I
and everybody else,

01:07:39.221 --> 01:07:41.057
and hopefully that
will be translated

01:07:41.098 --> 01:07:43.100
eventually into a legislation.

01:07:44.060 --> 01:07:47.063
[suspenseful music]

01:07:47.980 --> 01:07:49.398
[Narrator] Elon Musk's assertion

01:07:49.440 --> 01:07:53.027
that we're likely to be
living in a simulated reality

01:07:53.069 --> 01:07:56.489
has popularized a theory
debated by philosophers,

01:07:56.530 --> 01:07:59.784
and now gaining supporters
in Silicon Valley.

01:07:59.825 --> 01:08:01.285
Elon Musk has said

01:08:01.327 --> 01:08:04.872
that we're living in some kind
of computer, 3D simulation.

01:08:04.914 --> 01:08:06.916
I don't know if that
means we're in The Matrix,

01:08:06.957 --> 01:08:08.834
or some kind of a matrix.

01:08:08.876 --> 01:08:13.130
And you know, as as bizarre
and crazy as that sounds.

01:08:13.172 --> 01:08:15.132
Elon has been saying
bizarre and crazy things

01:08:15.174 --> 01:08:18.135
that turned out to be
true for a long time now,

01:08:18.177 --> 01:08:21.722
so, I would say um, maybe.

01:08:21.764 --> 01:08:23.724
Well, you never know do you?

01:08:23.766 --> 01:08:26.894
Well, disprove it,
we can't disprove it.

01:08:26.936 --> 01:08:28.979
We could be of a
lower intelligence,

01:08:29.021 --> 01:08:30.564
and there could
be another species

01:08:30.606 --> 01:08:33.776
far, far, far more
intelligent than us

01:08:33.818 --> 01:08:36.320
and they're almost
playing God with us.

01:08:36.362 --> 01:08:38.155
It sounds ridiculous to
say something like that,

01:08:38.197 --> 01:08:40.157
but we don't know much.

01:08:40.199 --> 01:08:42.660
We've barely left
our own planet.

01:08:42.701 --> 01:08:44.370
I felt like what
he was saying was,

01:08:44.411 --> 01:08:46.997
that this is not base reality.

01:08:47.039 --> 01:08:49.750
And so, I would agree
with him on that front.

01:08:49.792 --> 01:08:51.919
You get labeled crazy
because you're pushing

01:08:51.961 --> 01:08:55.005
the boundaries of
what people expect.

01:08:55.047 --> 01:08:58.467
He's coming at it from a
scientific perspective,

01:08:58.509 --> 01:09:01.762
but that idea, that there's
only one true reality,

01:09:01.804 --> 01:09:04.723
and this isn't it, is
actually the underpinnings

01:09:04.765 --> 01:09:07.852
of all the ancient
religions of the world.

01:09:07.893 --> 01:09:09.645
All of those point to exactly

01:09:09.687 --> 01:09:11.230
the same thing that he's saying,

01:09:11.272 --> 01:09:13.274
so I'm with him.

01:09:13.858 --> 01:09:16.026
I've been saying in my books,

01:09:16.068 --> 01:09:18.821
for a lot longer than Elon Musk,

01:09:18.863 --> 01:09:24.326
that we live in the equivalent
of a massively advanced

01:09:24.368 --> 01:09:27.580
virtual reality computer
game, in the end.

01:09:28.747 --> 01:09:35.504
And when you look at the
evidence, it is overwhelming,

01:09:35.546 --> 01:09:38.257
certainly immensely compelling,

01:09:38.299 --> 01:09:40.593
and this is why
not just Elon Musk,

01:09:40.634 --> 01:09:42.678
many scientists now are
coming out and saying,

01:09:42.720 --> 01:09:44.722
actually it does look like that.

01:09:44.763 --> 01:09:46.015
Ultimately for me it seems

01:09:46.056 --> 01:09:47.725
to be a bit of an
in-proveable theory.

01:09:47.766 --> 01:09:50.227
I mean how are we going to prove

01:09:50.269 --> 01:09:52.897
that we're living in a
simulation, I don't know.

01:09:52.938 --> 01:09:55.065
Don't discount his wacko ideas,

01:09:55.107 --> 01:10:00.029
because suddenly they do pop
up in one shape or another,

01:10:00.070 --> 01:10:02.573
but I'm not sure
about that one, heh.

01:10:02.615 --> 01:10:05.534
I think we need to see more
meat on the bone on that one.

01:10:05.576 --> 01:10:07.870
I think it's interesting that

01:10:07.912 --> 01:10:10.331
Musk is able to put
something like that out there

01:10:10.372 --> 01:10:12.958
and show that what
others might consider

01:10:13.000 --> 01:10:14.418
a bit of a ridiculous thought,

01:10:14.460 --> 01:10:16.837
or might be worried about
being ridiculed for,

01:10:16.879 --> 01:10:19.298
he's actually giving
serious thought to,

01:10:19.340 --> 01:10:21.592
and I think what Musk
is always looking to do

01:10:21.634 --> 01:10:23.969
is to foster a discussion.

01:10:26.263 --> 01:10:27.431
[horns honking]

01:10:27.473 --> 01:10:29.058
[Narrator] Inspired
by frustrations

01:10:29.099 --> 01:10:31.018
of sitting in a traffic jam,

01:10:31.060 --> 01:10:34.313
Musk's solution was
The Boring Company.

01:10:34.355 --> 01:10:38.400
In 2018, the company was
selected to build a high speed

01:10:38.442 --> 01:10:42.029
underground transportation
system in Chicago.

01:10:42.071 --> 01:10:44.949
I would like to thank the
Mayor and the Mayor's Office,

01:10:44.990 --> 01:10:46.992
and the city of Chicago
for having the faith

01:10:47.034 --> 01:10:49.536
to bet on The Boring Company.

01:10:51.330 --> 01:10:54.208
We think we're gonna really
be very good at boring.

01:10:54.250 --> 01:10:55.751
[audience laughing]

01:10:55.793 --> 01:10:57.586
You could almost say that
Elon has the Midas touch,

01:10:57.628 --> 01:10:59.964
because when he started
The Boring Company,

01:11:00.005 --> 01:11:02.508
which is really about
boring under the Earth

01:11:02.549 --> 01:11:04.051
as opposed to being dull,

01:11:04.093 --> 01:11:06.971
he actually started selling
baseball caps online

01:11:07.012 --> 01:11:08.264
before he did anything else,

01:11:08.305 --> 01:11:10.015
and literally made
a million dollars.

01:11:10.057 --> 01:11:12.309
You know, this is a guy
who everything he touches

01:11:12.351 --> 01:11:13.477
turns to gold.

01:11:13.519 --> 01:11:14.979
He's kept us all interested

01:11:15.020 --> 01:11:17.606
because he's
started tweeting out

01:11:17.648 --> 01:11:20.025
offers of hats
and flamethrowers.

01:11:20.067 --> 01:11:20.985
We were all interested to see

01:11:21.026 --> 01:11:22.611
what was gonna happen with it.

01:11:22.653 --> 01:11:24.655
This is a difficult
thing that we're doing.

01:11:24.697 --> 01:11:25.990
It's a hard thing.

01:11:26.031 --> 01:11:27.032
It's a new thing.

01:11:28.617 --> 01:11:33.622
And you, know I'd hope that
you'd cheer us on for this,

01:11:34.498 --> 01:11:36.166
you know, because if we succeed,

01:11:36.208 --> 01:11:38.210
it's gonna be a great
thing for the city,

01:11:38.252 --> 01:11:40.587
and if we if we fail, well,

01:11:40.629 --> 01:11:44.425
I guess me and others will
lose a bunch of money.

01:11:44.466 --> 01:11:46.302
[epic music]

01:11:46.343 --> 01:11:47.344
Elon Musk is one of the great

01:11:47.386 --> 01:11:49.972
engineering minds of our time.

01:11:50.014 --> 01:11:51.348
He is one of the
greatest business leaders

01:11:51.390 --> 01:11:53.350
and entrepreneurs of our time,

01:11:53.392 --> 01:11:56.145
and he is one of the great
visionaries of our time.

01:11:58.856 --> 01:12:00.566
Elon will be remembered
as one of the guys

01:12:00.607 --> 01:12:04.862
who was unafraid
to say, what else?

01:12:04.903 --> 01:12:07.948
I think his greatest achievement
is inspiring humanity

01:12:07.990 --> 01:12:10.659
to think bigger,
to dream bigger.

01:12:10.701 --> 01:12:13.120
In a world where there's
so many problems,

01:12:13.162 --> 01:12:16.040
isn't it nice to have a
positive story occasionally?

01:12:16.081 --> 01:12:18.334
We're all sort of just
sitting back and watching

01:12:18.375 --> 01:12:19.710
what's gonna come out next.

01:12:21.420 --> 01:12:24.131
[dramatic music]

